Like I took your quiz.

261
00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:37.766
I scored a 7.3.

262
00:11:37.766 --> 00:11:41.340
So I guess I'm prone to some level of bittersweetness,

263
00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.170
but I've always thought of myself as maybe

264
00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:47.520
just a little bit more sensitive than certain other people,

265
00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:50.010
and sensitivity could be a word that you could apply

266
00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:51.270
to introversion as well.

267
00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:54.840
Like they're just more sensitized to their environment or,

268
00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.330
I mean, is that a completely different

269
00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:58.590
way of thinking about this?

270
00:11:58.590 --> 00:11:59.700
Or how does that work out?

271
00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.890
No, it's more like these are all

272
00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:03.780
really overlapping categories.

273
00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:05.640
They don't lay totally on top of each other,

274
00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:06.750
but they overlap.

275
00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.630
So with bittersweetness, which by the way,

276
00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:13.980
I define as a kind of a state of mind where

277
00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:17.580
you're very attuned to the way in which

278
00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:21.000
joy and sorrow in this world are forever paired.

279
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.823
You don't get one without the other in this life,

280
00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.170
and the way in which everyone and everything we love

281
00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:29.940
will not be here forever.

282
00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:34.230
But that's somehow what comes with that knowledge,

283
00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:37.710
there comes a kind of deep joy at the beauty of the world.

284
00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:42.383
So it's like a real blend of all these deep instincts.

285
00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.710
And what we found, we have a bittersweet quiz.

286
00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:49.253
I say we, 'cause I developed it with Scott Barry Kaufman.

287
00:12:49.253 --> 00:12:50.086
I know you know.

288
00:12:50.086 --> 00:12:52.260
Yeah, I'm seeing him on Friday night.

289
00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:53.670
Oh, that's right, I know.

290
00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.130
He actually was inviting me to come,

291
00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:57.903
but I'll be back home by then.

292
00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.350
So Scott Barry Kaufman and I, and David Hiden,

293
00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.180
who's at John's Hopkins.

294
00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:07.740
We developed this quiz to measure how bittersweet you are.

295
00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:09.510
And what we found is that the people

296
00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:11.910
who score high on the quiz like you,

297
00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:15.300
also score high on this trade of high sensitivity

298
00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:17.610
that you're talking about kind of instinctively.

299
00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:22.140
And that trait is basically like the kind of person

300
00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.980
who just reacts much more intensely to everything.

301
00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:30.180
Like if you see the beauty of these canyons out here,

302
00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.550
like you're gonna really love it.

303
00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.700
And if there were suddenly loud and terrible noise,

304
00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:37.900
it would probably bother you that much more.

305
00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:43.320
And so many introverts are highly sensitive,

306
00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:44.760
but not all of them.

307
00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.610
So we actually didn't find that there's any correlation

308
00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:49.770
between bitter sweetness and introversion,

309
00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:51.182
the correlation is with sensitivity.

310
00:13:51.182 --> 00:13:52.230
Yeah, that's really interesting.

311
00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:53.063
Yeah, yeah.

312
00:13:53.063 --> 00:13:55.524
'Cause you would think that they would toggle together.

313
00:13:55.524 --> 00:13:58.350
Well, it's like about 80% of highly sensitive people

314
00:13:58.350 --> 00:13:59.640
are introverts,

315
00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:02.790
but you could be highly sensitive and be extroverted also.

316
00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:04.770
And then you could be introverted and not be sensitive.

317
00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:08.940
So that's why I say these are like overlapping categories.

318
00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.470
I'm interested in how we arrived

319
00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:15.180
at this moment in our culture,

320
00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:16.920
and you've written about this,

321
00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:19.590
where I wouldn't say we pathologize introversion,

322
00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:23.973
but we certainly don't like socially select for it.

323
00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:27.720
Introverts are sort of thought of as

324
00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:30.480
the people who are gonna have a harder time

325
00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:32.820
in the workplace achieving,

326
00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:36.300
they tend to have more difficulty getting their ideas across

327
00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:40.200
because of the constructs that we've created around

328
00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:45.180
education and professional careers.

329
00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.070
And you've written extensively about like

330
00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:48.420
how we've gotten to this point

331
00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.880
and how we might rethink these cultural morays

332
00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:53.970
or priorities to better understand,

333
00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:57.960
not just this dynamic between introversion and extroversion,

334
00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:00.450
but also like how to leverage the beautiful talents

335
00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:03.510
of the introverts so that they can thrive because,

336
00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:05.820
just like extroverts, they can become leaders.

337
00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.770
And they're often the people with the most

338
00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:11.460
interesting creative forward thinking ideas.

339
00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:13.410
Yeah, and so there's this real mismatch.

340
00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:16.200
I mean, when you look at who have been

341
00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:18.720
many of the most creative people over time,

342
00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.490
I mean, like psychologists have studied this,

343
00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:24.600
and you usually find people who are quite introverted

344
00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:26.430
or at least to some degree introverted,

345
00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:30.750
because solitude is such a key ingredient of creativity,

346
00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:32.580
which is not something that ever gets talked about,

347
00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:33.413
but it's true.

348
00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:36.393
Yeah, and for me,

349
00:15:39.946 --> 00:15:41.490
I think I was just aware of this mismatch

350
00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:42.720
from a really early age

351
00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:45.390
because I come from a family of introverts.

352
00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:47.010
Like I don't think we have a single extrovert

353
00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:48.630
in my family of origin.

354
00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:52.230
And it was just really plain to me that the different things

355
00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.813
that my family members were doing in the world,

356
00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:01.200
things that I admired were so related to

357
00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:04.200
their kind of quiet and cerebral way of being.

358
00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:07.350
I had a father who he was a doctor

359
00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:08.550
and a medical school professor,

360
00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:09.960
and he was amazing at what he did.

361
00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.310
And he was the one you'd go to

362
00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:13.230
if you couldn't figure out the diagnosis,

363
00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.120
he might be able to figure it out.

364
00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:19.650
And he was also somebody who would work these long hours

365
00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:21.210
and then he would come home after work

366
00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.523
and pour over medical journals,

367
00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:27.180
and like go to the medical conferences

368
00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:28.830
and sit in the front row and tape everything

369
00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:30.150
and listen to it over and over again

370
00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:31.964
until he'd figured it out.

371
00:16:31.964 --> 00:16:36.870
And it was things like that I kept seeing in real life,

372
00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:38.880
and then many of the artists I admired

373
00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:40.470
and writers I admired.

374
00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.270
So just like people out in the world

375
00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:46.290
were so clearly contributing, not in spite of,

376
00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:49.410
but because of their quieter way of being.

377
00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:51.590
And yet, as you're saying,

378
00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:54.300
in our schools and in our workplaces,

379
00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:55.740
it's not thought of that way.

380
00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:56.730
Right.

381
00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:00.570
And so what is the solution for that?

382
00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:04.140
I know you've done a lot of work around like reorienting

383
00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:07.710
the workplace and trying to find ways where we can

384
00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:11.790
foster that type of energy and bring it to the surface

385
00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:15.367
and in a way where it is respected better.

386
00:17:15.367 --> 00:17:17.640
I mean, I do think as with everything,

387
00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:21.990
that raising awareness is the hugest step of all.

388
00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:23.790
And it's actually been amazing to see

389
00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:25.500
how over the last 10 years,

390
00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.960
how much more awareness there is,

391
00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.710
and how much of a shift in orientation there is.

392
00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:33.480
So there are lots of workplaces that are like

393
00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.060
thinking about this in really conscious terms.

394
00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:38.730
And they have work in groups formed around it.

395
00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:41.580
And they're thinking through different ways of hiring

396
00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:43.950
and promotion and that kind of thing.

397
00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:45.880
And you also see schools doing things

398
00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:51.960
like rethinking the way kids are given feedback.

399
00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:53.793
'Cause so many kids are told,

400
00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:59.280
like Sophie is doing great academically,

401
00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.310
but she must learn to speak up more in class.

402
00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:06.000
And getting that feedback compared to,

403
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:10.260
Sophie is a deep thinker and when she contributes,

404
00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:12.070
everybody turns around to listen

405
00:18:13.475 --> 00:18:16.470
because she has so many interesting insights to add.

406
00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.630
That feels completely different to Sophie.

407
00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.450
And I've gotten a lot of letters from Sophies of the world

408
00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:24.210
telling me of how painful it is to them

409
00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:26.340
when they get the first type of feedback.

410
00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:29.250
They feel like their teachers don't respect them at all.

411
00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:31.950
So I am seeing those kinds of changes.

412
00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:33.810
We are seeing those kinds of changes,

413
00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:36.030
and that's really gratifying,

414
00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.063
even though I think we have a long way to go.

415
00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.740
I just know for myself, when I think about

416
00:18:43.853 --> 00:18:47.880
open floor plan workplaces or co-working place

417
00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:49.470
that sounds terrible to me.

418
00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:52.380
Like I want a womb, I wanna shut the door.

419
00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:53.880
I don't want any windows.

420
00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:56.040
I don't want anybody to ever knock on that door

421
00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.210
or for the phone to ring.

422
00:18:57.210 --> 00:19:00.300
Like my great ambition is to be left alone.

423
00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:02.100
And when I can inhabit that place,

424
00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.280
I then can do the work that I feel compelled to do,

425
00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:06.333
my best work.

426
00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.880
But anything that involves collisions or like inter,

427
00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:13.380
just sounds like a nightmare to me.

428
00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:17.700
And when I see that trend of like these open floor plans,

429
00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.910
like that doesn't seem conducive to the introvert,

430
00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:24.120
like being able to express themselves optimally.

431
00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:25.500
Oh, my God, yes.

432
00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:28.410
And in fact, I discovered this because,

433
00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:30.510
well, like you, I used to be a corporate lawyer.

434
00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:32.250
So I had come from this world,

435
00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:34.290
I assume your firm was like this where

436
00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:35.940
everyone had their own little office.

437
00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.550
It was almost like a dorm room or a dormitory

438
00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:40.620
with everybody in their office.

439
00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:43.140
So I kind of thought that's what work looked like.

440
00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.120
And then I started researching "Quiet."

441
00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.180
So I decided I was gonna like plop down in Silicon valley

442
00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.013
for a while,

443
00:19:49.013 --> 00:19:51.370
because I figured there would be many introverts there.

444
00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:56.130
And I was expecting this kind of introvert Nirvana.

445
00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:57.390
When I got there,

446
00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:59.730
and all these people who I was interviewing,

447
00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:02.490
they're all coming up to me and kind of whispering like,

448
00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:06.030
this open office plan that I'm in is a disaster,

449
00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:07.080
and I can't concentrate.

450
00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.460
Like everything that you just said.

451
00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:09.600
Everything's overwhelming.

452
00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:10.560
Everything's overwhelming.

453
00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.300
And they were like, I can't tell my boss.

454
00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:13.260
This is why they're whispering.

455
00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.720
They're like, I can't tell my boss because

456
00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:17.040
if my boss heard me saying this,

457
00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:18.840
they would think I'm not a team player.

458
00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:20.400
They think I don't like my colleagues.

459
00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.460
And it's not that it's just, it's everything you just said

460
00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:28.320
about the best way that they would feel would focus

461
00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:29.910
and be in a state of equilibrium

462
00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.713
would be in private environments.

463
00:20:33.833 --> 00:20:35.100
So they start asking me like,

464
00:20:35.100 --> 00:20:37.470
is there research that I could show my boss

465
00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:40.140
to just let them rethink this a little bit?

466
00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:41.403
And I started looking,

467
00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:46.110
and this was like a long time ago.

468
00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:49.020
But even by then, there was a lot of research.

469
00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:51.600
There was a mountain of research showing all the problems

470
00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.900
with these open office plans specifically for introverts,

471
00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:56.520
but really for everyone.

472
00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:00.060
Focus goes down, and people get more sick, all this stuff.

473
00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:02.310
So fast forward,

474
00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:05.070
I would say now people are starting to understand

475
00:21:05.070 --> 00:21:10.020
how problematic that kind of floor plan is.

476
00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:13.230
And especially now in the wake of the pandemic,

477
00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:15.600
there is a kind of rethinking going on.

478
00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:17.340
Where we end up remains to be seen,

479
00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:20.160
because it's also much more cost efficient

480
00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:22.025
to have everybody crammed into one big space.

481
00:21:22.025 --> 00:21:23.370
Sure, of course.

482
00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:26.700
The pandemic must have provided you with a lot of data

483
00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:29.370
on this, like on one level,

484
00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:32.010
it's sort of Nirvana for the introvert who now

485
00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:34.050
can be (indistinct) in their own home

486
00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:36.390
and can sort of control their environments

487
00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:40.050
a little bit better, but then over time, maybe not so good.

488
00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:42.837
Like what have you learned about the pandemic in terms of

489
00:21:42.837 --> 00:21:45.020
the introversion extroversion thing

490
00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:47.190
in the workplace, et cetera.

491
00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:49.340
It's a little bit of a mixed bag because,

492
00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:53.700
as you're suggesting, there's a way in which,

493
00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.010
especially during kind of peak lockdown,

494
00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.110
there's a way in which it was much easier for

495
00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:00.860
your typical introvert than for your typical extrovert,

496
00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:06.420
but all the uncertainty that was involved and the disruption

497
00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:10.023
isn't easy for anyone and introverts in general,

498
00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:14.400
are less comfortable with uncertainty than extroverts are.

499
00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:17.910
So I would say it took a toll in different kinds of ways,

500
00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:19.533
but what I really saw is that,

501
00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:23.583
excuse me, especially at that moment,

502
00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:27.093
it was really a fantastic time,

503
00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:31.350
regardless of who you were to take stock,

504
00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:32.850
or regardless of your temperament,

505
00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:35.550
to take stock of whether your previous life

506
00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:37.830
had been working for you or not.

507
00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:41.370
'Cause, I knew many extroverts who were telling me,

508
00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:42.570
you know what, even for me,

509
00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:45.960
I was going to 24/7 and now I realize that

510
00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:47.523
I wanna pull this back.

511
00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:51.180
And then there were introverts who were saying,

512
00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:52.320
this is fantastic.

513
00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:54.330
And I love not having to go to the office all the time

514
00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:59.193
and I have to rework my life to preserve some of this.

515
00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:02.440
Yeah, I think

516
00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:07.080
we're gonna be learning about this for years to come.

517
00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:11.700
Like we're still kind of in it and we don't necessarily have

518
00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:15.450
the 10,000 foot view on the long term impact of this,

519
00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:18.360
but it's been hard for everybody.

520
00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:21.540
It's been very hard on you and your family, I know that.

521
00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.940
And must have been excruciating for you

522
00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:26.700
while writing this book on bitter sweetness to,

523
00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:28.773
kind of endure loss in your own family.

524
00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:32.670
Yeah, that was an interesting thing because

525
00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:37.670
people assume that I wrote "Bittersweet,"

526
00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:40.950
like in response to the disruptions

527
00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:42.753
and the losses of the pandemic,

528
00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:46.860
because as you say, I lost my brother first

529
00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:49.770
and then my father to COVID,

530
00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:51.900
but I was actually working on this book

531
00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:53.520
for years before that.

532
00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.670
That is my way, I work on things for a really long time.

533
00:23:56.670 --> 00:24:01.620
So I don't know,

534
00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:03.570
what's interesting to me about that is,

535
00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:08.867
I see the world as bittersweet all the time,

536
00:24:08.867 --> 00:24:10.860
and I'm like, regardless of what's going on,

537
00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:14.160
so when it's a pandemic, it's not surprising to me.

538
00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:16.950
And the epigraph of the book comes from

539
00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:20.520
the Leonard Cone quote of "There's a crack in everything"

540
00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:22.440
and that's where the light gets in.

541
00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:26.160
So because of seeing the world that way,

542
00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:30.033
it means when we're in difficult times,

543
00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:35.190
I'm also more apt to be looking for what the light side is

544
00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:37.320
just as when we're in good times, I'm like,

545
00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:39.360
come on, you guys, don't you realize everything is not

546
00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:41.670
as perfect as you think.

547
00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:43.440
There's also all this loss and fragility

548
00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:44.733
going on at every moment.

549
00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:47.340
It's a kind of worldview

550
00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:49.440
where you're seeing it all constantly.

551
00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:53.175
Well, it's a non-binary non-dualistic approach, right?

552
00:24:53.175 --> 00:24:56.970
It's a very holistic approach to how emotions operate,

553
00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.280
and you can't have one without the other,

554
00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:03.060
and this is a soup and the more that we can appreciate

555
00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.610
that one doesn't happen without the other,

556
00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:09.060
the more kind of space there is for healing

557
00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:13.560
and kind of understanding the beauty and the power

558
00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:16.560
of leaning into these emotions rather than avoiding them,

559
00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:20.310
because we're constantly being signaled to just be happy

560
00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:23.740
or pursue happiness at all times, at all costs.

561
00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:24.813
Right, right.

562
00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:27.690
Yeah, and in fact,

563
00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:33.360
I gave a talk about bitter sweetness at Ted the summer

564
00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:35.280
before the pandemic hit.

565
00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:37.590
So it was the summer of 2019.

566
00:25:37.590 --> 00:25:40.590
And it was really interesting because,

567
00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:42.600
I felt like there were half the people,

568
00:25:42.600 --> 00:25:45.120
like when I came offstage and I knew a whole bunch of people

569
00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:47.580
in the audience and half of them were like,

570
00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:48.930
oh my gosh, this is totally me,

571
00:25:48.930 --> 00:25:50.820
and I totally understand what you're talking about.

572
00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:51.930
And then some of them were like,

573
00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:55.200
oh, I didn't know that you were depressive.

574
00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:56.033
And I was like,

575
00:25:56.033 --> 00:25:58.000
no, that's actually not what I'm saying at all.

576
00:25:59.743 --> 00:26:00.576
And that's

577
00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:06.570
part of the problem that our culture has is that

578
00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:09.510
we don't have a way of distinguishing between,

579
00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:10.590
clinical depression,

580
00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:12.900
which I would never recommend to anybody,

581
00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:17.733
versus this way of being that takes it all in,

582
00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:20.790
the good and the bad and is just aware of it all.

583
00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:21.623
Right.

584
00:26:21.623 --> 00:26:23.550
It's very telling that somebody would just leap to that.

585
00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:24.600
Yeah.

586
00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:26.910
And associate those two things as being the same,

587
00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:28.590
and pathologizing it, right?

588
00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:30.840
Oh my, I'm sorry, something's wrong with you,

589
00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:33.060
as opposed to this is life.

590
00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:36.180
And of course we aspire to live happy lives,

591
00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:41.180
but that happiness is informed by the inevitable obstacles

592
00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:45.870
and painful moments that descend upon all of us

593
00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:48.600
and the manner in which we're able to kind of

594
00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:52.050
embrace those moments, learn from them, lean into them,

595
00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:55.470
I think enriches the happier moments because

596
00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:57.840
that's truly how you appreciate life

597
00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:00.120
rather than running away from those other things

598
00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.940
and repressing them and trying to dodge them.

599
00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:04.830
Yeah, and that there's this whole...

600
00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:07.140
Like if you look at all our wisdom traditions

601
00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:11.730
and our literary and artistic heritages,

602
00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:13.620
across the world, across the centuries,

603
00:27:13.620 --> 00:27:17.310
like they have been teaching all this time that

604
00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:19.120
this is a way in that

605
00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:23.340
that appreciating this side of existence,

606
00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:27.150
the bittersweet side of existence is a way to creativity,

607
00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:29.943
it's a way to connection, it's a way to transcendence.

608
00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:34.770
And the way that I got into this whole inquiry

609
00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:36.660
in the first place is because,

610
00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:40.620
all my life I had had this really intense reaction

611
00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:42.450
to sad music.

612
00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:44.340
But by intense, I don't mean intensely sad.

613
00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:46.170
I mean like I'd hear this music

614
00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:48.123
and feel completely uplifted.

615
00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:52.440
So there's this one moment I was in law school,

616
00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:55.590
and some friends were picking me up in my law school dorm

617
00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:56.910
on the way to class.

618
00:27:56.910 --> 00:28:00.720
And I was blasting Leonard Cohen or something,

619
00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:02.043
as I want to do.

620
00:28:03.398 --> 00:28:05.640
And my friends came by and they thought it was hilarious.

621
00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:06.473
And they were like,

622
00:28:06.473 --> 00:28:08.730
why are you listening to this funeral music?

623
00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:12.450
And at the time I just laughed and went to class.

624
00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:14.790
And that was the end of the story.

625
00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:19.790
But I couldn't stop thinking about it because it was like,

626
00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:22.650
well, what is it in our culture that makes it so odd

627
00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:24.930
to listen to this music, but even more,

628
00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:27.273
what is it that the music is telling us?

629
00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:30.330
There's something in it.

630
00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:35.280
I mean, we know that sad music is much more likely

631
00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:38.550
to produce like goosebumps and chills and listeners,

632
00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.190
people listen to the happy songs on their playlists

633
00:28:41.190 --> 00:28:44.793
175 times, but they listen to the sad songs 800 times.

634
00:28:46.694 --> 00:28:49.503
And there's somewhere that that music is pulling us,

635
00:28:49.503 --> 00:28:53.700
it's like that the musician is telling you

636
00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:55.200
that thing that you felt,

637
00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:57.270
that pain that you've sometimes felt,

638
00:28:57.270 --> 00:28:59.160
I, the musician have been there too.

639
00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:01.500
And everybody listening to this has been there too.

640
00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:04.110
And we are all connected in this same

641
00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:06.033
strange state of being human.

642
00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:09.570
And that's where the feelings of uplift and love come from.

643
00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:11.238
Right, and connection of course&gt;

644
00:29:11.238 --> 00:29:12.071
And connection.

645
00:29:12.071 --> 00:29:14.430
You feel like you're seen and you're being heard.

646
00:29:14.430 --> 00:29:17.340
There is somebody else there through a musical note

647
00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.770
can identify very specifically in emotion

648
00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:20.880
that you're experiencing

649
00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:23.010
and suddenly you don't feel alone anymore.

650
00:29:23.010 --> 00:29:23.843
Yeah, exactly.

651
00:29:23.843 --> 00:29:25.620
And that's a joyful experience,

652
00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:29.580
even if it's one that also is kind of

653
00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:32.220
about sorrow and pain and grief.

654
00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:34.650
Right, because I think the ultimate human desire

655
00:29:34.650 --> 00:29:37.530
is for connection, and that's what it's doing.

656
00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:39.780
And so, you look at our culture now

657
00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:42.690
and there's so much divisiveness and so on.

658
00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:46.320
And I don't think that's unrelated to the way in which

659
00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:47.650
we're supposed to present

660
00:29:49.756 --> 00:29:52.623
so cheerfully and so successfully all the time,

661
00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:57.322
that means we're not really able to connect with each other.

662
00:29:57.322 --> 00:29:58.200
Right.

663
00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:00.570
Right, 'cause we're fronted and we're wearing that mask

664
00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:01.893
and we're trying to,

665
00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:06.060
appear or present in a certain way to be approved of,

666
00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:10.440
or to be received in a manner that we would desire,

667
00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:15.420
and that ultimately just alienates us from one another.

668
00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:17.430
Yeah, exactly, exactly.

669
00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:19.205
I tend to look at these things,

670
00:30:19.205 --> 00:30:20.790
and I've said this many times before,

671
00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:23.190
as somebody who's been sober for a long time,

672
00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:26.520
through the lens of recovery and,

673
00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:28.890
the more that I was reading your book,

674
00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:30.870
the more I couldn't help but think about

675
00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:34.200
how this operates in the 12 step context,

676
00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:39.070
because you're part of this community that has

677
00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:42.510
kind of a shared narrative about

678
00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:43.830
the pain that they've endured

679
00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:46.350
and the struggles that they've sort of overcome

680
00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:48.360
to be in this place.

681
00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:51.510
But then somebody has the courage to get up in front of

682
00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:54.330
a group of people and share their pain

683
00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:56.190
and their grief and their sorrow,

684
00:30:56.190 --> 00:30:58.050
but do it with a level of levity

685
00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:01.860
and also a level of specificity.

686
00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:04.230
And I found the more specific it is,

687
00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:05.763
the more universal it is.

688
00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:10.500
I may not relate to the facts of that person's experience,

689
00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:12.870
but I can relate to the emotional experience

690
00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:15.000
that they've had and you can't help,

691
00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:18.300
but feel incredibly bonded to this person

692
00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:19.920
and to the group,

693
00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:22.410
through the courage, and the pain, and the grief,

694
00:31:22.410 --> 00:31:25.110
and the levity that makes it all this

695
00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:29.040
soupy mess of emotions

696
00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:31.503
that just make you feel more human.

697
00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:34.583
Yeah.

698
00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:38.400
As you were describing that,

699
00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:43.320
what came into my mind is Darwin actually,

700
00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:45.153
'cause Darwin was actually,

701
00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:50.220
he was a really kind of gentle and melancholic

702
00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:51.900
type of person.

703
00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:54.270
So he's known for survival of the fittest,

704
00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:56.280
but he was actually this guy who like,

705
00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:57.990
he couldn't stay on the site of blood.

706
00:31:57.990 --> 00:31:59.610
His father wanted him to be a doctor,

707
00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:02.370
and he took one look at like what surgery looked like

708
00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:07.053
and fled to the jungles of the Galapagos to look at Beatles.

709
00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:12.840
And he noticed from very early on,

710
00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:16.530
I mean, he was very aware of how cruel and violent

711
00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:18.990
animals and humans could be with each other,

712
00:32:18.990 --> 00:32:22.210
but he also noticed at the same time that

713
00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:26.370
there's this impulse that mammals and humans have

714
00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:29.130
that when they see another being in distress,

715
00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:31.290
that they feel the distress themselves,

716
00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:33.300
it just gets mirrored and it's happening

717
00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:36.303
at a really like quick and pre-conscious level.

718
00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:38.670
And he thought that that was

719
00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:41.370
the strongest impulse of all in humans.

720
00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:43.560
So people talk about how

721
00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:46.260
he's known for survival of the fittest but,

722
00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:48.910
you could equally talk about survival of the kindest.

723
00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:53.913
And now we're seeing the fruits of that,

724
00:32:53.913 --> 00:32:56.280
like 150 years later in the research,

725
00:32:56.280 --> 00:33:00.030
like there's a guy named Dr. Kelner at Berkeley,

726
00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:01.590
who does all this amazing research.

727
00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:04.770
And he's found for example, that we all have a Vagus nerve,

728
00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.170
which is the most important,

729
00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:09.600
biggest bundle of nerves in our bodies,

730
00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:11.850
regulates breathing and digestion.

731
00:33:11.850 --> 00:33:16.390
And your Vagus nerve becomes activated

732
00:33:17.757 --> 00:33:20.430
when you were in your AA group

733
00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:23.193
and seeing somebody talk about their troubles,

734
00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:26.370
if you felt that sense of like your heart opening up

735
00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:28.830
or your throat closing or whatever it is,

736
00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:32.850
that's partly your Vagus nervous is becoming activated

737
00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:34.260
almost against your will.

738
00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:35.553
It just happens.

739
00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:43.770
And so to cut that off from each other,

740
00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:46.320
the ability to relate to each other that way,

741
00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:49.740
is cutting off one of our most basic ways of bonding.

742
00:33:49.740 --> 00:33:51.870
Right, so there is this implicit,

743
00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:56.870
like evolutionary advantage to indulging

744
00:33:56.910 --> 00:34:00.603
in this kind of emotional landscape.

745
00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:04.563
Yeah, I mean, even the word indulging,

746
00:34:05.511 --> 00:34:06.900
I wouldn't think of it...

747
00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:08.490
Allowing, yeah.

748
00:34:08.490 --> 00:34:09.990
To allowing, exactly.

749
00:34:09.990 --> 00:34:13.200
I mean, it actually comes from our need as mammals

750
00:34:13.200 --> 00:34:14.970
to be able to take care of our young,

751
00:34:14.970 --> 00:34:17.700
like you have to take care of a baby who's crying

752
00:34:17.700 --> 00:34:20.250
and that's how they let you know that they need something

753
00:34:20.250 --> 00:34:22.920
because we're primed to be able to do that,

754
00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:24.990
that kind of radiates outward to our ability

755
00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:27.327
to react to each other in general, in that way.

756
00:34:27.327 --> 00:34:28.650
And we don't always get it right

757
00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:31.890
but that is one deep aspect of being human.

758
00:34:31.890 --> 00:34:34.800
And then neurochemistry tracks to the vagus nerve.

759
00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:36.120
Yeah, exactly.

760
00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:39.390
Which makes me think this is like deeply primal

761
00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:40.620
dating all the way back.

762
00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:41.820
Exactly, exactly.

763
00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:44.250
And the vagus nerve is like one of the most ancient parts

764
00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:45.878
of the human body.

765
00:34:45.878 --> 00:34:48.610
So it's really fascinating.

766
00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:53.013
Yeah, this idea of bitter sweetness,

767
00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:57.570
I think most people think of it as an experience,

768
00:34:57.570 --> 00:35:00.390
but you talk about it as like a state of being.

769
00:35:00.390 --> 00:35:04.260
So distinguish those two things and how you arrived at that

770
00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:05.093
point of view.

771
00:35:05.093 --> 00:35:05.926
Yeah.

772
00:35:05.926 --> 00:35:08.493
So I mean, it is an experience in the sense of,

773
00:35:08.493 --> 00:35:10.920
like the moment where you're walking your child

774
00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:12.870
down the aisle or something,

775
00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:15.930
that is a quintessential bittersweet experience.

776
00:35:15.930 --> 00:35:18.450
But there's also a view of the world

777
00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:21.363
and a way of being in the world, that's quite bittersweet.

778
00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:24.990
You could call it melancholic, except that word

779
00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:28.050
in our culture is associated with clinical depression.

780
00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:30.090
And that's not really what it means,

781
00:35:30.090 --> 00:35:31.740
or it's not the way I'm using it.

782
00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:37.233
So the bittersweet state of being is much more about,

783
00:35:42.106 --> 00:35:44.610
it's a sense of that awareness of joy and sorrow,

784
00:35:44.610 --> 00:35:45.933
and of fragility.

785
00:35:47.130 --> 00:35:49.113
Aristotle 2000 years ago,

786
00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:53.580
he has this question of why is it that

787
00:35:53.580 --> 00:35:57.720
so many of our great poets, philosophers and politicians,

788
00:35:57.720 --> 00:35:59.730
all have a melancholic temperament?

789
00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:01.890
Like what is that?

790
00:36:01.890 --> 00:36:06.690
So it's something about being attuned to the gap

791
00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:10.290
between the world as it is,

792
00:36:10.290 --> 00:36:12.603
and the world as we would wish it to be.

793
00:36:15.780 --> 00:36:18.330
The emotional DNA of humans is like,

794
00:36:18.330 --> 00:36:20.880
we come into this world with a sense that

795
00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:22.650
there is a more perfect and beautiful world

796
00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.200
that's out there somewhere to which we belong,

797
00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:27.660
but we somehow find ourselves here instead.

798
00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:29.040
Right.

799
00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:31.620
It's the yearning and the longing,

800
00:36:31.620 --> 00:36:33.540
like the idea that things could be better.

801
00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:35.280
It's not a despairing per se,

802
00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:38.880
but it's a sense that like we're in a certain place,

803
00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:40.053
it could be better.

804
00:36:41.065 --> 00:36:44.730
And the melancholic impulse is built out of like,

805
00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:46.890
how to get from one to the other.

806
00:36:46.890 --> 00:36:50.250
Yeah, and it's the heart of our creative impulse,

807
00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:54.660
it's like a feeling of like, how do you get closer to that

808
00:36:54.660 --> 00:36:59.040
perfect and beautiful world for which you were yearning.

809
00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:02.910
The word longing, like it literally means to grow longer

810
00:37:02.910 --> 00:37:04.590
and to reach for.

811
00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:08.580
So there is a sense in which we are all reaching

812
00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:11.160
to get to this other place.

813
00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:14.220
And that's what propels us forward.

814
00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:15.990
Like, historically, we've always known this,

815
00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:18.240
it's really only recently that we've forgotten it.

816
00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:20.553
So with the Odyssey Homer,

817
00:37:22.539 --> 00:37:25.530
that whole adventure, it starts with Homer,

818
00:37:25.530 --> 00:37:29.280
who's on a beach weeping for his homeland,

819
00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:32.790
'cause he's so homesick and because he's so homesick,

820
00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:35.290
he goes out into the water and has this adventure.

821
00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:39.600
It's the same thing with all our religions,

822
00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:41.310
like we're longing for the garden of Eden,

823
00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:44.070
we're longing for Mecca, we're longing for Zion,

824
00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.860
and all the religions that like the teaching is that

825
00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:49.410
it's through the longing that you get closer

826
00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:51.423
to the divine itself.

827
00:37:52.260 --> 00:37:55.620
So it's an incredibly generative state.

828
00:37:55.620 --> 00:38:00.620
Right, and it's about temporality as well.

829
00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:04.020
This idea of fragility and nothing lasts forever.

830
00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:06.750
And we're only here for a short period of time,

831
00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:10.713
and that ticking clock, kind of Memento mori,

832
00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.120
is sort of a driver behind this state as well.

833
00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:16.890
Absolutely, absolutely.

834
00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:20.370
I actually tried practicing Memento mori,

835
00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:21.570
while I was writing the book.

836
00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:26.220
So like Memento mori, it basically means remembering death,

837
00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:29.070
as a way of like understanding how precious life is,

838
00:38:29.070 --> 00:38:33.030
and that idea comes to us from many different traditions.

839
00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:35.220
So I tried it and I actually found it

840
00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:37.713
to be so incredibly helpful.

841
00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:41.793
And the place I really noticed it is like,

842
00:38:42.690 --> 00:38:44.760
my kids at the time were pretty little

843
00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:47.260
and we had this bedtime ritual that we were doing.

844
00:38:48.798 --> 00:38:50.310
And it was an amazing time of day.

845
00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:53.250
And it was like reliably the time that they would open up

846
00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:56.760
about whatever was on their minds, and it was just great.

847
00:38:56.760 --> 00:39:01.080
And I also was bringing my cell phone into the room

848
00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:04.170
while I did that, while we would do bedtime.

849
00:39:04.170 --> 00:39:05.283
I would say to myself,

850
00:39:06.750 --> 00:39:09.570
they may not be here tomorrow, you may not be here tomorrow.

851
00:39:09.570 --> 00:39:11.100
You have no idea.

852
00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:14.850
And just that thought

853
00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:17.970
would completely extinguish any desire I had

854
00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:19.797
to look at the cell phone, like was gone.

855
00:39:19.797 --> 00:39:22.347
And I stopped bringing it into the room completely.

856
00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:26.640
And it didn't feel sad,

857
00:39:26.640 --> 00:39:29.760
it didn't feel depressing, it was just a reminder.

858
00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:31.080
Right.

859
00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:34.980
That for me, connects to gratitude,

860
00:39:34.980 --> 00:39:36.310
like if I can be present

861
00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:39.150
that's what I'm trying to cultivate.

862
00:39:39.150 --> 00:39:42.090
It's not necessarily a bitter sweetness other than like,

863
00:39:42.090 --> 00:39:42.923
oh my goodness,

864
00:39:42.923 --> 00:39:45.393
if this is the last time that I'm able to do this,

865
00:39:46.481 --> 00:39:48.881
there's a dusting of sadness on that, I suppose.

866
00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.240
It's complicated.

867
00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:52.312
It's complicated.

868
00:39:52.312 --> 00:39:53.145
Like how to tease all this stuff out.

869
00:39:53.145 --> 00:39:56.220
No wonder it took you so long to write this book.

870
00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:58.440
Just trying to wrap my head around like what it is,

871
00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:00.633
and what it isn't, how you cultivate it,

872
00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:04.593
how to channel it appropriately.

873
00:40:05.580 --> 00:40:07.440
Well, do you know the feeling I'm talking about

874
00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:08.940
when you listen,

875
00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:10.230
I don't know what kind of music you love.

876
00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:11.413
I mean, we all like many different kinds but-

877
00:40:11.413 --> 00:40:14.490
I love Leonard Cohen and I know he's your spirit animal.

878
00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:16.770
He's my spirit animal, it's true.

879
00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:18.650
Yeah, no, I mean, it's just like...

880
00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.240
I started out with that question of why sad music,

881
00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:25.437
like what's so great about it?

882
00:40:25.437 --> 00:40:27.780
And that seemed like a kind of narrow question.

883
00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:30.660
I didn't really realize it was gonna become a whole book.

884
00:40:30.660 --> 00:40:33.430
It was more that was a gateway question

885
00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:36.570
into this whole state.

886
00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:38.700
Right, but if you look at the musicians,

887
00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:43.170
the great poets, the writers, all of the painters,

888
00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:45.840
the ones that seem to resonate the most

889
00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:48.340
are the ones who have

890
00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:51.780
the capacity to kind of take these

891
00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:53.940
seemingly conflicting emotional states

892
00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:58.620
or the polarities of them and weave them in some way

893
00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:00.660
that makes sense to us as humans,

894
00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:03.960
but perhaps transcends our ability to like articulate.

895
00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:05.430
And I think when you see it,

896
00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:07.140
it's sort of like when you hear it, you know it,

897
00:41:07.140 --> 00:41:08.490
when you see it, you know it.

898
00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:11.610
You're not sure like why is it that the minor key

899
00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:15.390
or these certain songs and the way that they're constructed,

900
00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:17.070
cultivate that in ourselves?

901
00:41:17.070 --> 00:41:19.110
I don't know if there's neurochemistry on that

902
00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:20.910
or any science on that,

903
00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:23.370
but it really is like, I know what that is,

904
00:41:23.370 --> 00:41:24.900
I don't know why I know what that is.

905
00:41:24.900 --> 00:41:28.350
It makes me feel a certain way that is,

906
00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:32.730
perhaps seemingly off my optimal state.

907
00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:34.830
And yet there's a comfort.

908
00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:37.200
Like you talk about rainy days and things like that.

909
00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:39.660
Like you kind of wanna languish in it.

910
00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:43.680
Yeah, there's a comfort and there's a transcendence in it.

911
00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:47.850
Leonard Cohen, the very first artistic act that he took

912
00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:50.350
was when he was nine years old and his father died

913
00:41:53.416 --> 00:41:57.570
and he took one of his father's bow ties and he wrote a poem

914
00:41:57.570 --> 00:42:00.760
and he buried the bow tie and the poem

915
00:42:01.889 --> 00:42:04.680
in the backyard garden of their family house.

916
00:42:04.680 --> 00:42:06.930
And that was his first artistic act.

917
00:42:06.930 --> 00:42:09.720
And it was like, he was kind of repeating that act

918
00:42:09.720 --> 00:42:11.970
again and again, throughout his career,

919
00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:16.050
there's a kind of like taking something painful

920
00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:18.390
and then turning it into something else.

921
00:42:18.390 --> 00:42:22.380
And I think that's like the real, in terms of how to live,

922
00:42:22.380 --> 00:42:26.130
that's the real insight that this tradition gives us,

923
00:42:26.130 --> 00:42:28.503
because it's kind of saying to us,

924
00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:33.240
these moments are gonna come, these pains are gonna come.

925
00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:35.160
You have two choices of what to do with them.

926
00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:39.420
You can suppress it in some way and inevitably,

927
00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:42.060
you're gonna take it out on yourself

928
00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:44.700
in the form of depression or addiction or whatever it is,

929
00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:46.590
or you're gonna take it out on someone else,

930
00:42:46.590 --> 00:42:48.720
abuse, passive aggression,

931
00:42:48.720 --> 00:42:53.720
or you can take those pains and sit with them somehow

932
00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:58.260
and then make meaning out of them and transform them.

933
00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:01.173
And that's something that we do so naturally.

934
00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:05.010
Like after 9/11,

935
00:43:05.010 --> 00:43:06.840
you suddenly see all these people signing up

936
00:43:06.840 --> 00:43:09.690
to be firefighters, the spike of applications,

937
00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:10.980
it goes way up.

938
00:43:10.980 --> 00:43:13.620
After the pandemic, lots of medical school

939
00:43:13.620 --> 00:43:15.840
and nursing school applications.

940
00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:17.343
So people are like,

941
00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:20.575
you would think they might be running away from things-

942
00:43:20.575 --> 00:43:22.620
Towards the thing that is creating the pain.

943
00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:24.030
Exactly, exactly.

944
00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:24.863
But it's a way

945
00:43:27.044 --> 00:43:29.550
of making sense out of it and making meaning of it,

946
00:43:29.550 --> 00:43:30.990
and turning it into something better.

947
00:43:30.990 --> 00:43:34.260
And I think that's one of our fundamental impulses

948
00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:35.370
when we're at our best.

949
00:43:35.370 --> 00:43:38.670
Right, like take your pain and transmute it,

950
00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:40.500
turn it into a creative offering.

951
00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:43.350
Yeah, and I use the word creative,

952
00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:45.960
I mean, in a very good way.

953
00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:47.340
I don't pain to painting.

954
00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:50.010
Spend seven years writing a book, it can be anything.

955
00:43:50.010 --> 00:43:51.030
Yeah, exactly.

956
00:43:51.030 --> 00:43:54.033
Yeah, and that's really powerful and I've thought,

957
00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:57.930
that made me reflect on my own life and realize that

958
00:43:57.930 --> 00:44:01.350
without being consciously aware of it, like I've done that.

959
00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:04.140
So there is something instinctual in us

960
00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:06.123
that's drawn to that practice.

961
00:44:09.390 --> 00:44:11.040
Like before you were in recovery,

962
00:44:11.040 --> 00:44:13.480
do you think you were taking the other path or like?

963
00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:17.220
Oh, I mean, before when I was indulging in my addiction,

964
00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:19.890
no, I was running away from everything and repressing

965
00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:23.640
all of those challenging emotions and medicating myself,

966
00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:27.180
essentially, because those emotions were too painful

967
00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:32.100
or I wasn't mature enough to really excavate them.

968
00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:36.570
And I was at the behest of a powerful substance.

969
00:44:36.570 --> 00:44:37.403
Yeah.

970
00:44:37.403 --> 00:44:38.433
That it hijacked me.

971
00:44:40.069 --> 00:44:42.990
And I think most recovering addicts,

972
00:44:42.990 --> 00:44:44.250
will be able to relate to that.

973
00:44:44.250 --> 00:44:45.810
And I think there's something interesting about

974
00:44:45.810 --> 00:44:48.723
the recovery community and what you kind of learn is that,

975
00:44:49.770 --> 00:44:52.710
there's something aspirational there, even in the attic,

976
00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:54.520
like they're looking for

977
00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:58.680
a higher experience of self and consciousness,

978
00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:00.900
and they're doing it in a very self-destructive

979
00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:02.610
and unhealthy way.

980
00:45:02.610 --> 00:45:06.570
But at the very core of it is like,

981
00:45:06.570 --> 00:45:08.940
there should be something better or I'm in pain,

982
00:45:08.940 --> 00:45:11.793
and I'm reaching for something else.

983
00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:16.500
And then when you get sober and you can continue to reach

984
00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:19.410
in a healthier direction, people in recovery,

985
00:45:19.410 --> 00:45:21.390
like I know so many people,

986
00:45:21.390 --> 00:45:23.430
they're just amazing human beings,

987
00:45:23.430 --> 00:45:28.230
because they've learned to take that pain and turn it into

988
00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:30.190
some form of creative offering

989
00:45:31.650 --> 00:45:33.630
that is healing for not only themselves,

990
00:45:33.630 --> 00:45:35.433
but for a lot of other people.

991
00:45:37.162 --> 00:45:38.190
(upbeat music)

992
00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:39.903
Prophets walk among us,

993
00:45:40.830 --> 00:45:44.370
as a writer and podcaster for nearly 10 years,

994
00:45:44.370 --> 00:45:46.410
I've become more convinced than ever,

995
00:45:46.410 --> 00:45:49.020
that our world is populated by scores

996
00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:51.300
