when we're reminded of our mortality,

1300
00:59:39.353 --> 00:59:41.580
that in the immediate aftermath of being reminded of it,

1301
00:59:41.580 --> 00:59:45.090
we become much more like focused on our in groups

1302
00:59:45.090 --> 00:59:47.970
and hostile to out groups.

1303
00:59:47.970 --> 00:59:51.600
So there's like studies where they'll remind people

1304
00:59:51.600 --> 00:59:54.420
of their mortality and then ask them how much hot sauce

1305
00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:56.550
they wanna put on the food that they're gonna give to

1306
00:59:56.550 --> 00:59:58.710
people in the opposing political party.

1307
00:59:58.710 --> 01:00:01.530
And people will put on too much hot sauce

1308
01:00:01.530 --> 01:00:05.332
'cause they get into this sort of anti out group mode.

1309
01:00:05.332 --> 01:00:07.230
So they take that and they say,

1310
01:00:07.230 --> 01:00:09.720
well, this means that

1311
01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:12.570
when we solve the problem of mortality,

1312
01:00:12.570 --> 01:00:15.270
we're also gonna be solving all our other problems

1313
01:00:15.270 --> 01:00:17.610
of conflict and war and everything else, 'cause it's like,

1314
01:00:17.610 --> 01:00:20.880
if we could solve this, we'll solve everything else.

1315
01:00:20.880 --> 01:00:22.800
And that's where I feel like,

1316
01:00:22.800 --> 01:00:24.750
I meant there's ways in which I'm actually

1317
01:00:24.750 --> 01:00:28.620
really sympathetic to the life extension project.

1318
01:00:28.620 --> 01:00:32.590
But I do feel like it's kind of missing

1319
01:00:33.840 --> 01:00:36.120
the larger point

1320
01:00:36.120 --> 01:00:40.350
that our problems are not only about the fact of mortality,

1321
01:00:40.350 --> 01:00:42.960
the problems are much deeper than that.

1322
01:00:42.960 --> 01:00:46.770
They have to do with

1323
01:00:46.770 --> 01:00:49.800
just all the longings of existence that would be here,

1324
01:00:49.800 --> 01:00:51.690
whether or not we lived forever.

1325
01:00:51.690 --> 01:00:52.523
Yeah.

1326
01:00:52.523 --> 01:00:54.720
Like they're still there, we're not gonna sidestep that.

1327
01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:58.560
And I think, of course there's something aspirational

1328
01:00:58.560 --> 01:01:00.480
about tackling this problem,

1329
01:01:00.480 --> 01:01:02.550
and it's fascinating to learn about it

1330
01:01:02.550 --> 01:01:04.920
and there have been these amazing breakthroughs in science,

1331
01:01:04.920 --> 01:01:05.753
et cetera.

1332
01:01:06.840 --> 01:01:10.320
But I can't help but think whether or not it's being driven

1333
01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:13.680
by just a deep seated fear of death

1334
01:01:13.680 --> 01:01:16.320
that's so desperate that we must solve this problem

1335
01:01:16.320 --> 01:01:19.770
because I don't wanna have to face that myself.

1336
01:01:19.770 --> 01:01:21.810
Well, I think they would actually,

1337
01:01:21.810 --> 01:01:24.000
in some ways agree with that.

1338
01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:25.890
I mean, part of the reason that I went

1339
01:01:28.200 --> 01:01:31.020
to see them and to study them was 'cause I was interested

1340
01:01:31.020 --> 01:01:32.700
just in this culture that we live in,

1341
01:01:32.700 --> 01:01:35.640
that wants everything to be like,

1342
01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:36.960
winning and sunshine all the time.

1343
01:01:36.960 --> 01:01:38.880
And I thought this would be a really interesting

1344
01:01:38.880 --> 01:01:40.620
example of that.

1345
01:01:40.620 --> 01:01:43.860
But what I actually found is all the scientists

1346
01:01:43.860 --> 01:01:46.230
would get up on stage and present their thing.

1347
01:01:46.230 --> 01:01:49.410
They would begin their scientific presentation

1348
01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:51.870
usually by telling a really heart winding story

1349
01:01:51.870 --> 01:01:54.865
of a person that they had lost in their lives,

1350
01:01:54.865 --> 01:01:57.450
and of how harrowing and how terrible

1351
01:01:57.450 --> 01:01:58.773
the bereavement had been.

1352
01:01:59.970 --> 01:02:02.220
And so they were like quite explicitly saying,

1353
01:02:02.220 --> 01:02:04.290
they were like, this is so terrible.

1354
01:02:04.290 --> 01:02:07.090
We just as a society should not be tolerating

1355
01:02:08.430 --> 01:02:10.860
this deep pain, we should be doing something about it.

1356
01:02:10.860 --> 01:02:12.750
So there was a way in which I was like really moved

1357
01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:16.920
because I felt like they were doing their own version of

1358
01:02:16.920 --> 01:02:19.260
taking a pain and trying to transform it

1359
01:02:19.260 --> 01:02:20.610
into something else.

1360
01:02:20.610 --> 01:02:24.363
And their something else is immortality itself.

1361
01:02:28.590 --> 01:02:32.193
Yeah, and what they would say, I think to your point is,

1362
01:02:34.020 --> 01:02:39.020
well, the idea that we have that there's a way in which

1363
01:02:39.420 --> 01:02:41.733
death or mortality gives us meaning to life,

1364
01:02:41.733 --> 01:02:44.970
that that's like a nice story that we tell ourselves

1365
01:02:44.970 --> 01:02:46.200
because we have no choice.

1366
01:02:46.200 --> 01:02:47.730
But if we really did have a choice,

1367
01:02:47.730 --> 01:02:49.547
then we might not be telling that story anymore.

1368
01:02:49.547 --> 01:02:51.257
Right, right, right.

1369
01:02:53.760 --> 01:02:55.410
And I'm not so sure about that.

1370
01:02:56.310 --> 01:02:57.300
Time will tell.

1371
01:02:58.380 --> 01:03:01.680
But it's funny that you say 300 years, 'cause I feel like,

1372
01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:05.381
300 years, that kind of sounds sort of plausible to me.

1373
01:03:05.381 --> 01:03:06.683
'Cause if you think about it,

1374
01:03:07.800 --> 01:03:11.430
we would have life expectancies of 35 or 40,

1375
01:03:11.430 --> 01:03:13.200
now it's 70 or 80 or whatever it is,

1376
01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:16.623
and we're totally comfortable with having doubled it.

1377
01:03:16.623 --> 01:03:19.470
But that's different from the achieving,

1378
01:03:19.470 --> 01:03:20.303
what is it called?

1379
01:03:20.303 --> 01:03:23.730
Life expectancy velocity or escape velocity.

1380
01:03:23.730 --> 01:03:24.690
Oh, yeah.

1381
01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:26.550
What is that longevity philosophy.

1382
01:03:26.550 --> 01:03:27.480
Something like that.

1383
01:03:27.480 --> 01:03:30.300
Where just basically every year you live like,

1384
01:03:30.300 --> 01:03:34.321
the extends the ability to extend the life.

1385
01:03:34.321 --> 01:03:35.154
Right, right.

1386
01:03:35.154 --> 01:03:36.600
Fortunately more or whatever.

1387
01:03:36.600 --> 01:03:39.060
So by definition

1388
01:03:39.060 --> 01:03:41.730
that means you could potentially live forever.

1389
01:03:41.730 --> 01:03:44.850
Right, theoretically, yeah.

1390
01:03:44.850 --> 01:03:46.233
We'll see, I don't know.

1391
01:03:47.130 --> 01:03:48.450
But on that subject,

1392
01:03:48.450 --> 01:03:52.680
that's not a distant cousin from this idea of transcendence.

1393
01:03:52.680 --> 01:03:56.940
And so I'm interested in how this dovetails into

1394
01:03:56.940 --> 01:04:01.440
a discussion around spirituality and religion,

1395
01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:05.640
these traditions of religion that relate to bittersweetness

1396
01:04:05.640 --> 01:04:10.167
that kind of percolate across all these different variations

1397
01:04:13.320 --> 01:04:15.750
of practice, I guess.

1398
01:04:15.750 --> 01:04:17.670
'Cause that's super interesting.

1399
01:04:17.670 --> 01:04:21.240
There is something (indistinct) about all of this.

1400
01:04:21.240 --> 01:04:22.647
Oh yeah, absolutely.

1401
01:04:22.647 --> 01:04:25.740
And I had no idea when I started off on this path

1402
01:04:25.740 --> 01:04:28.260
that I was gonna end up writing and thinking

1403
01:04:28.260 --> 01:04:29.643
so much about religion,

1404
01:04:30.810 --> 01:04:33.270
but I started realizing this whole subject is like

1405
01:04:33.270 --> 01:04:36.190
intricately bound up with that because

1406
01:04:37.320 --> 01:04:39.090
almost every religion that I see,

1407
01:04:39.090 --> 01:04:42.750
especially the mystical sides of our religions,

1408
01:04:42.750 --> 01:04:45.570
they're all talking about this state of longing

1409
01:04:45.570 --> 01:04:48.870
that all beings have for

1410
01:04:48.870 --> 01:04:50.883
union with a divine, essentially.

1411
01:04:52.290 --> 01:04:54.630
And the idea that the state of longing is actually

1412
01:04:54.630 --> 01:04:55.980
healthy in that way.

1413
01:04:55.980 --> 01:04:59.400
Like Rumi, the 12th century, Sufi poet,

1414
01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:01.230
he says, be thirsty.

1415
01:05:01.230 --> 01:05:05.040
And that the longing you express...

1416
01:05:05.040 --> 01:05:07.140
Well, he has this one poem where he is talking

1417
01:05:08.310 --> 01:05:12.030
about a man who's praying to Allah,

1418
01:05:12.030 --> 01:05:14.550
and he doesn't get an answer back.

1419
01:05:14.550 --> 01:05:18.840
And so he becomes kind of bitter and disappointed

1420
01:05:18.840 --> 01:05:20.163
and he stops praying.

1421
01:05:22.201 --> 01:05:24.930
And then he falls into asleep and he's not sleeping well.

1422
01:05:24.930 --> 01:05:28.560
And Hitter who's the guide of souls comes along to him,

1423
01:05:28.560 --> 01:05:30.810
and says, "Why did you stop praying?"

1424
01:05:30.810 --> 01:05:33.567
And the man says, "'Cause I never got an answer."

1425
01:05:36.117 --> 01:05:37.897
And the guide of soul says to him,

1426
01:05:37.897 --> 01:05:42.183
"The longing that you express that is the return message.

1427
01:05:43.117 --> 01:05:44.827
"And the grief that you cry out from

1428
01:05:44.827 --> 01:05:47.367
"is exactly what draws you towards union."

1429
01:05:48.360 --> 01:05:51.450
And so this is the message that we get again and again,

1430
01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:52.560
from these traditions,

1431
01:05:52.560 --> 01:05:55.170
like the grief is what draws you towards

1432
01:05:55.170 --> 01:05:56.770
the ultimate love that you seek.

1433
01:05:58.770 --> 01:05:59.793
And the yearning,

1434
01:06:00.840 --> 01:06:03.990
it's almost like a spiritual or a Boris though,

1435
01:06:03.990 --> 01:06:06.630
because these things are a circle,

1436
01:06:06.630 --> 01:06:07.710
they're a cycle, right?

1437
01:06:07.710 --> 01:06:09.300
Like the yearning,

1438
01:06:09.300 --> 01:06:13.157
the answer that you seek is in the yearning itself.

1439
01:06:13.157 --> 01:06:15.450
Yeah, yeah.

1440
01:06:15.450 --> 01:06:16.440
It's really beautiful.

1441
01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:17.880
Yeah, and I don't think...

1442
01:06:17.880 --> 01:06:18.713
Well, I don't know.

1443
01:06:18.713 --> 01:06:20.187
I mean, maybe someone does, I wouldn't know,

1444
01:06:20.187 --> 01:06:23.330
but I don't know that we ever like actually get there

1445
01:06:23.330 --> 01:06:24.163
on this earth,

1446
01:06:24.163 --> 01:06:28.050
but there's something about the act of drawing closer

1447
01:06:28.050 --> 01:06:30.840
that is intensely satisfying.

1448
01:06:30.840 --> 01:06:31.860
Yeah.

1449
01:06:31.860 --> 01:06:35.340
And how did that exploration land for you as somebody

1450
01:06:35.340 --> 01:06:37.950
who kind of identifies as agnostic,

1451
01:06:37.950 --> 01:06:41.223
but you had this grandfather who was this beautiful rabbi.

1452
01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:43.920
Where did this lead you?

1453
01:06:43.920 --> 01:06:46.410
Well, it was really interesting for me because I grew...

1454
01:06:46.410 --> 01:06:48.630
Yeah, my grandfather was an Orthodox rabbi

1455
01:06:48.630 --> 01:06:51.690
and like, I really adored him, everybody did.

1456
01:06:51.690 --> 01:06:55.920
And he was like such a huge figure in my life.

1457
01:06:55.920 --> 01:06:59.043
And at the same time, I've always been deeply agnostic.

1458
01:07:00.630 --> 01:07:03.930
And didn't really honestly think that much about religion

1459
01:07:03.930 --> 01:07:05.370
once I became an adult,.

1460
01:07:05.370 --> 01:07:08.343
I was kind of dismissive of it, if anything.

1461
01:07:13.800 --> 01:07:15.790
I don't know, I started to realize that

1462
01:07:21.448 --> 01:07:24.780
the thing that I feel when I listen to sad music

1463
01:07:24.780 --> 01:07:26.067
and what someone else might feel,

1464
01:07:26.067 --> 01:07:28.020
and I do to some extent when I'm in nature

1465
01:07:28.020 --> 01:07:29.777
or something like that,

1466
01:07:29.777 --> 01:07:32.220
the way I see it is that they're all manifestations

1467
01:07:32.220 --> 01:07:34.470
of the same experience.

1468
01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:37.650
And for some people, they have that experience

1469
01:07:37.650 --> 01:07:40.020
through the language of the divine and religion.

1470
01:07:40.020 --> 01:07:43.350
And for others, they have it through music or art or sports,

1471
01:07:43.350 --> 01:07:45.300
or whatever it is, but we're all kind of reaching

1472
01:07:45.300 --> 01:07:46.770
for that same state.

1473
01:07:46.770 --> 01:07:49.203
And in fact, there's this (indistinct) parable

1474
01:07:49.203 --> 01:07:53.223
that I came across that talks about,

1475
01:07:54.066 --> 01:07:57.060
that there's a rabbi and he has a man in his congregation

1476
01:07:57.060 --> 01:07:59.553
who he notices is paying no attention at all.

1477
01:08:01.950 --> 01:08:04.290
And isn't really buying what the rabbi's saying.

1478
01:08:04.290 --> 01:08:08.670
And then the rabbi hums for the man a yearning melody,

1479
01:08:08.670 --> 01:08:11.280
a bittersweet melody, and the man listens.

1480
01:08:11.280 --> 01:08:12.367
And he says,

1481
01:08:12.367 --> 01:08:14.647
"Now I understand what you've been trying to tell me

1482
01:08:14.647 --> 01:08:17.197
"all this this time, because I'm feeling this intense,

1483
01:08:17.197 --> 01:08:19.737
"longing to be United with God."

1484
01:08:20.760 --> 01:08:24.510
And I read that and I was like, oh my God, I'm that old man.

1485
01:08:24.510 --> 01:08:25.343
Yeah.

1486
01:08:26.670 --> 01:08:28.140
That's Leonard Cohen for you.

1487
01:08:28.140 --> 01:08:29.280
Yeah.
Right.

1488
01:08:29.280 --> 01:08:33.720
And what is it about that minor key or that

1489
01:08:33.720 --> 01:08:38.400
musical sensibility that elevates the soul

1490
01:08:38.400 --> 01:08:41.310
and can't help but leave you wondering

1491
01:08:41.310 --> 01:08:45.180
whether there is something more beyond the senses.

1492
01:08:45.180 --> 01:08:47.280
Yeah, I think it's because it's touching

1493
01:08:48.289 --> 01:08:51.580
that sense that we all have of there being

1494
01:08:53.520 --> 01:08:55.860
some other place that we're reaching for.

1495
01:08:55.860 --> 01:08:58.980
You see it in like the "Wizard of Oz," right?

1496
01:08:58.980 --> 01:09:02.700
It's like, Dorothy's longing for somewhere over the rainbow.

1497
01:09:02.700 --> 01:09:04.380
You look at all our children's stories.

1498
01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:07.380
There's always the protagonist who enters the scene,

1499
01:09:07.380 --> 01:09:09.570
at the moment they become an orphan,

1500
01:09:09.570 --> 01:09:11.520
that's when the whole adventure begins.

1501
01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:13.650
Like the moment they're thrust into

1502
01:09:13.650 --> 01:09:15.153
the state of primal belonging.

1503
01:09:18.517 --> 01:09:21.330
So I think that's just tapping into a DNA that we all share,

1504
01:09:21.330 --> 01:09:24.233
although it manifests differently for each of us.

1505
01:09:25.522 --> 01:09:29.790
There's this idea that this shared proclivity towards

1506
01:09:29.790 --> 01:09:32.550
bitter sweetness allows us to understand

1507
01:09:32.550 --> 01:09:35.160
that we are one super organism,

1508
01:09:35.160 --> 01:09:39.390
but perhaps it extends beyond the vagus nerve, right?

1509
01:09:39.390 --> 01:09:40.711
Yeah.

1510
01:09:40.711 --> 01:09:41.544
Right, right.

1511
01:09:41.544 --> 01:09:44.340
And you who like you're exploring Judaism and Sufism,

1512
01:09:44.340 --> 01:09:49.340
and these various religions and mystical traditions.

1513
01:09:49.530 --> 01:09:52.110
And there are similarities that

1514
01:09:52.110 --> 01:09:53.610
they're very different in many ways,

1515
01:09:53.610 --> 01:09:56.370
but there are these similarities that unite them.

1516
01:09:56.370 --> 01:10:00.273
And from that, can one extrapolate,

1517
01:10:01.500 --> 01:10:05.730
some truth about the beyond or our relationship with

1518
01:10:05.730 --> 01:10:09.633
something more ethereal and non-material.

1519
01:10:10.500 --> 01:10:11.333
Yeah.

1520
01:10:12.930 --> 01:10:15.030
I don't know that exploring all this gives

1521
01:10:15.930 --> 01:10:18.810
definitive answers to any of these lifelong questions,

1522
01:10:18.810 --> 01:10:20.583
but for me,

1523
01:10:22.980 --> 01:10:26.670
it offers a kind of roadmap of how to live,

1524
01:10:26.670 --> 01:10:30.390
because what it does is it makes you notice

1525
01:10:30.390 --> 01:10:35.390
how incredibly miraculous everything around us actually is,

1526
01:10:35.880 --> 01:10:38.070
how sacred it really is,

1527
01:10:38.070 --> 01:10:39.420
whether you're an atheist or believer,

1528
01:10:39.420 --> 01:10:42.543
I'm just using those words, like the wonder of it all.

1529
01:10:43.560 --> 01:10:45.990
And you can enter into a relationship

1530
01:10:45.990 --> 01:10:47.490
with that kind of wonder.

1531
01:10:47.490 --> 01:10:51.840
That really it's a complete enhancement,

1532
01:10:51.840 --> 01:10:54.300
a really transformation of what life is like.

1533
01:10:54.300 --> 01:10:55.410
It's a better way to live.

1534
01:10:55.410 --> 01:10:57.715
It's a much better way to live.

1535
01:10:57.715 --> 01:10:59.460
The good news is you can cultivate it

1536
01:10:59.460 --> 01:11:01.590
in whatever tradition suits you.

1537
01:11:01.590 --> 01:11:03.150
Absolutely, absolutely.

1538
01:11:03.150 --> 01:11:05.400
There are so many paths towards that.

1539
01:11:05.400 --> 01:11:07.710
Yeah, and we actually found in that bittersweet quiz

1540
01:11:07.710 --> 01:11:10.980
that we did that people who score high in bittersweetness

1541
01:11:10.980 --> 01:11:13.890
also score high in states of

1542
01:11:13.890 --> 01:11:16.953
awe and wonder and spirituality.

1543
01:11:17.910 --> 01:11:18.840
It's not surprising.

1544
01:11:18.840 --> 01:11:20.340
It's not surprising.

1545
01:11:21.761 --> 01:11:23.813
What is the relationship between bittersweetness

1546
01:11:23.813 --> 01:11:24.840
and awe and wonder,

1547
01:11:24.840 --> 01:11:27.633
they're like two sides of a similar coin.

1548
01:11:29.040 --> 01:11:29.940
Are they not?

1549
01:11:29.940 --> 01:11:31.983
Yeah, they are because,

1550
01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:37.960
basically, they're both born of

1551
01:11:41.250 --> 01:11:45.780
a receptivity and a noticing of everything that is.

1552
01:11:45.780 --> 01:11:48.390
So you're not only noticing one side of what is,

1553
01:11:48.390 --> 01:11:51.780
you're like looking for the truth of everything,

1554
01:11:51.780 --> 01:11:53.580
and that's what gets you into that state.

1555
01:11:53.580 --> 01:11:55.170
Yeah, when I think of awe and wonder,

1556
01:11:55.170 --> 01:11:56.520
I think of being present,

1557
01:11:56.520 --> 01:11:58.860
like if you're present enough,

1558
01:11:58.860 --> 01:12:01.170
and you just see the leaf on the tree,

1559
01:12:01.170 --> 01:12:02.910
that could inspire awe and wonder,

1560
01:12:02.910 --> 01:12:06.160
but it requires like a level of attention

1561
01:12:07.410 --> 01:12:08.970
that kind of alludes,

1562
01:12:08.970 --> 01:12:10.560
the way that we live our lives, right?

1563
01:12:10.560 --> 01:12:14.220
Like you have to really, really be practiced in that

1564
01:12:14.220 --> 01:12:16.170
or habitual in it

1565
01:12:16.170 --> 01:12:18.300
There's another parable that I came across

1566
01:12:18.300 --> 01:12:21.633
that I found incredibly useful as a way to live.

1567
01:12:22.710 --> 01:12:24.927
And this one also came from the Kalaba.

1568
01:12:24.927 --> 01:12:26.770
And it's the idea that

1569
01:12:27.630 --> 01:12:30.660
all of creation was originally

1570
01:12:30.660 --> 01:12:33.630
this intact divine vessel.

1571
01:12:33.630 --> 01:12:36.000
It was all intact, and then it broke,

1572
01:12:36.000 --> 01:12:37.320
the vessel broke.

1573
01:12:37.320 --> 01:12:39.240
And the world that we're living in now

1574
01:12:39.240 --> 01:12:42.030
is the world after the breakage.

1575
01:12:42.030 --> 01:12:44.850
And yet the shards of this vessel

1576
01:12:44.850 --> 01:12:47.520
are scattered everywhere around us.

1577
01:12:47.520 --> 01:12:49.080
And so what we can do,

1578
01:12:49.080 --> 01:12:50.910
we're still living in the broken world,

1579
01:12:50.910 --> 01:12:53.310
but we're living in a world where you can bend down

1580
01:12:53.310 --> 01:12:56.073
and pick up these shards of light.

1581
01:12:57.180 --> 01:12:59.230
And I love this as a way to live because

1582
01:13:00.360 --> 01:13:04.740
it's like a way of making sense of the tragedies

1583
01:13:04.740 --> 01:13:08.310
that are still an always part of this world,

1584
01:13:08.310 --> 01:13:10.920
but yet being able to turn in the direction

1585
01:13:10.920 --> 01:13:14.640
of joy and beauty, because you're not denying the tragedies,

1586
01:13:14.640 --> 01:13:17.700
you're not believing that a utopia is gonna come,

1587
01:13:17.700 --> 01:13:20.430
but you're still able to bend down and pick up

1588
01:13:20.430 --> 01:13:22.320
the shards of light that you personally notice.

1589
01:13:22.320 --> 01:13:24.720
And we're all gonna notice different ones,

1590
01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:27.540
but they're yours for the taking, yours to pick up.

1591
01:13:27.540 --> 01:13:30.815
Yeah, that's really beautiful.

1592
01:13:30.815 --> 01:13:33.030
But there's also packed into that a little bit of

1593
01:13:33.030 --> 01:13:36.390
a lament of what once was, or could have been,

1594
01:13:36.390 --> 01:13:37.860
had it not shattered.

1595
01:13:37.860 --> 01:13:39.300
That's true.

1596
01:13:39.300 --> 01:13:42.360
But I don't know a way to live without that lament,

1597
01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:45.120
when you look at the tragedies in the world,

1598
01:13:45.120 --> 01:13:48.240
like you have to make space for the lament also.

1599
01:13:48.240 --> 01:13:49.073
Sure.

1600
01:13:49.073 --> 01:13:50.820
Otherwise you're just living with blinders, I think.

1601
01:13:50.820 --> 01:13:52.263
Yeah, yeah.

1602
01:13:53.160 --> 01:13:58.160
Is this attunement attunement to bittersweetness trainable?

1603
01:14:00.090 --> 01:14:02.580
Like you can take the bittersweet test

1604
01:14:02.580 --> 01:14:04.200
and see where you score on it.

1605
01:14:04.200 --> 01:14:07.110
And certain people are obviously more attuned

1606
01:14:07.110 --> 01:14:09.540
or receptive to this than others,

1607
01:14:09.540 --> 01:14:13.740
but to the extent that appreciating bitter sweetness

1608
01:14:13.740 --> 01:14:18.000
or cultivating it, or kind of synthesizing

1609
01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:20.070
all the different colors of your experience

1610
01:14:20.070 --> 01:14:22.230
into some kind of creative offering

1611
01:14:22.230 --> 01:14:24.720
and making sense of it for yourself,

1612
01:14:24.720 --> 01:14:27.360
seems to be something that anybody can do.

1613
01:14:27.360 --> 01:14:32.360
And even if they score lower on that test,

1614
01:14:32.370 --> 01:14:34.800
they could increase their score.

1615
01:14:34.800 --> 01:14:35.973
And perhaps like,

1616
01:14:38.280 --> 01:14:42.788
elevate the richness of their life experience in so doing.

1617
01:14:42.788 --> 01:14:45.870
Yeah, so there are gonna be some people

1618
01:14:45.870 --> 01:14:48.360
who are born into the world

1619
01:14:48.360 --> 01:14:50.100
with that high sensitivity trait,

1620
01:14:50.100 --> 01:14:51.300
and they're probably gonna tend

1621
01:14:51.300 --> 01:14:53.610
in this bittersweet direction from the beginning,

1622
01:14:53.610 --> 01:14:55.470
then there's gonna be a lot of people who

1623
01:14:55.470 --> 01:14:57.600
just by virtue of life experience,

1624
01:14:57.600 --> 01:15:01.390
come to more of a bittersweet awareness over time

1625
01:15:02.580 --> 01:15:04.200
once they've-

1626
01:15:04.200 --> 01:15:05.033
Things happen in their life.

1627
01:15:05.033 --> 01:15:05.866
Things happen.

1628
01:15:05.866 --> 01:15:08.110
Jar 'em out of their lack of empathy or whatever it is.

1629
01:15:08.110 --> 01:15:10.830
Yeah, exactly, exactly.

1630
01:15:10.830 --> 01:15:14.130
And for people who aren't there yet but would wanna be,

1631
01:15:14.130 --> 01:15:17.100
I think it's just a matter of turning your awareness

1632
01:15:17.100 --> 01:15:18.210
in your direction...

1633
01:15:18.210 --> 01:15:21.010
I'm sorry, turning your awareness in certain directions.

1634
01:15:22.830 --> 01:15:24.540
That's scary though.

1635
01:15:24.540 --> 01:15:27.930
Well, it's scary if you think that by doing that,

1636
01:15:27.930 --> 01:15:29.610
you'll never come out again.

1637
01:15:29.610 --> 01:15:31.020
And that's what I hear from a lot of people.

1638
01:15:31.020 --> 01:15:33.000
They're like, I'm afraid if I go there,

1639
01:15:33.000 --> 01:15:34.400
I won't be able to come out.

1640
01:15:39.330 --> 01:15:41.940
And yet there's no reason that that should be so

1641
01:15:41.940 --> 01:15:45.810
because these two states are always paired, right?

1642
01:15:45.810 --> 01:15:46.710
It's joy and sorrow.

1643
01:15:46.710 --> 01:15:49.260
So there's no reason you have to stay in one state.

1644
01:15:49.260 --> 01:15:52.380
It's more just a sense of being able to tell the truth

1645
01:15:52.380 --> 01:15:55.020
about everything that's happening or everything you observe.

1646
01:15:55.020 --> 01:15:56.250
Yeah.

1647
01:15:56.250 --> 01:15:57.083
The fear-

1648
01:15:57.083 --> 01:15:58.560
But why do you think it's scary?

1649
01:15:58.560 --> 01:16:00.690
I mean, I just think you're asking people

1650
01:16:00.690 --> 01:16:04.230
to confront painful past emotions

1651
01:16:04.230 --> 01:16:07.110
or to look objectively at stuff

1652
01:16:07.110 --> 01:16:09.600
that they've kind of worked hard to build walls around.

1653
01:16:09.600 --> 01:16:11.970
So that prospect of deconstructing those walls,

1654
01:16:11.970 --> 01:16:15.420
and really grappling with some past trauma

1655
01:16:15.420 --> 01:16:17.820
or something that occurred or whatever,

1656
01:16:17.820 --> 01:16:21.570
that you've kind of really tried hard to like move away from

1657
01:16:21.570 --> 01:16:25.080
requires a certain level of courage,

1658
01:16:25.080 --> 01:16:27.060
and I think it is frightening for a lot of people.

1659
01:16:27.060 --> 01:16:29.810
Like I know a lot of people who have built walls around

1660
01:16:31.500 --> 01:16:33.330
certain pains in their life

1661
01:16:33.330 --> 01:16:37.140
and by dint of that are able to kind of safely navigate

1662
01:16:37.140 --> 01:16:39.330
through their life but they wanna stay in that lane.

1663
01:16:39.330 --> 01:16:40.890
If you say, listen,

1664
01:16:40.890 --> 01:16:43.170
you're missing a huge growth opportunity

1665
01:16:43.170 --> 01:16:46.980
or a certain level of enriching life experience

1666
01:16:46.980 --> 01:16:49.050
by ignoring that, you should look at it.

1667
01:16:49.050 --> 01:16:50.130
And if you work through it,

1668
01:16:50.130 --> 01:16:51.780
like your life is gonna open up in ways

1669
01:16:51.780 --> 01:16:53.460
you can't possibly imagine.

1670
01:16:53.460 --> 01:16:55.290
Intellectually, they may understand that,

1671
01:16:55.290 --> 01:17:00.150
and I can kind of see that arc awaiting that person.

1672
01:17:00.150 --> 01:17:02.280
But for a lot of people, it's like,

1673
01:17:02.280 --> 01:17:05.550
that's fine, I hear you, but I'm not gonna do that.

1674
01:17:05.550 --> 01:17:06.383
And you've had that.

1675
01:17:06.383 --> 01:17:10.650
I mean, your story about your mom, on a certain level,

1676
01:17:10.650 --> 01:17:12.000
like illustrates this for me.

1677
01:17:12.000 --> 01:17:15.460
Like what I read in that was a certain

1678
01:17:18.660 --> 01:17:20.760
kind of affect in your mother,

1679
01:17:20.760 --> 01:17:24.690
she had her own painful experiences growing up,

1680
01:17:24.690 --> 01:17:28.560
and that very much informed how she interrelated with you

1681
01:17:28.560 --> 01:17:30.480
at times in very unhealthy ways.

1682
01:17:30.480 --> 01:17:33.990
But could you have been able to get your mother

1683
01:17:33.990 --> 01:17:36.090
to kind of confront her past trauma

1684
01:17:36.090 --> 01:17:36.990
so that you could have had

1685
01:17:36.990 --> 01:17:38.400
a healthier relationship with her?

1686
01:17:38.400 --> 01:17:40.983
That seems like a very steep mountain decline.

1687
01:17:42.630 --> 01:17:45.480
Yeah, no, I think people are at different points

1688
01:17:45.480 --> 01:17:47.610
along where they are in their lives,

1689
01:17:47.610 --> 01:17:50.430
and sometimes you're not ready at a certain moment

1690
01:17:50.430 --> 01:17:51.933
and you are 10 years later.

1691
01:17:55.170 --> 01:17:56.930
And I guess another thing...

1692
01:18:00.150 --> 01:18:01.080
Sorry, just to go back,

1693
01:18:01.080 --> 01:18:02.580
that's an interesting question with my mom,

1694
01:18:02.580 --> 01:18:03.880
'cause I've often thought,

1695
01:18:08.580 --> 01:18:12.480
would there have been a way to have her be open to that

1696
01:18:12.480 --> 01:18:16.740
if I had thought about how to get there, to do that.

1697
01:18:16.740 --> 01:18:17.760
Can you explain a little bit,

1698
01:18:17.760 --> 01:18:19.290
like tell a little bit of that story.

1699
01:18:19.290 --> 01:18:20.373
The story, yeah.

1700
01:18:22.302 --> 01:18:25.240
So I had a really

1701
01:18:26.880 --> 01:18:29.940
intensely close relationship with my mother growing up.

1702
01:18:29.940 --> 01:18:33.210
It was a kind of garden of Eden type of childhood.

1703
01:18:33.210 --> 01:18:37.440
She was incredibly loving, devoted, warm,

1704
01:18:37.440 --> 01:18:40.593
amazing company, we really loved each other's company.

1705
01:18:42.900 --> 01:18:44.733
Yeah, it was really quite wondrous.

1706
01:18:46.050 --> 01:18:48.810
But my mother had passed trauma was of her own,

1707
01:18:48.810 --> 01:18:53.810
and this made it so that when I became an adolescent,

1708
01:18:54.000 --> 01:18:56.250
she had a really terrible time,

1709
01:18:56.250 --> 01:18:58.050
especially because I was the youngest child.

1710
01:18:58.050 --> 01:19:01.500
She had a really terrible time with my growing up,

1711
01:19:01.500 --> 01:19:04.740
and becoming independent and having different ideas

1712
01:19:04.740 --> 01:19:06.363
and different life experiences,

1713
01:19:08.782 --> 01:19:12.133
and her reaction to that was incredibly difficult,

1714
01:19:14.460 --> 01:19:15.633
to say the least.

1715
01:19:17.751 --> 01:19:19.050
And I had a lot of trouble with it too.

1716
01:19:19.050 --> 01:19:22.560
Like I felt like I didn't use these words and in those days,

1717
01:19:22.560 --> 01:19:24.840
but it was a kind of like casting out of the garden of Eden.

1718
01:19:24.840 --> 01:19:27.420
Like suddenly the mother that I had known all my life

1719
01:19:27.420 --> 01:19:31.893
had transformed into a hostile person.

1720
01:19:33.150 --> 01:19:34.200
Where there was kind of a choice,

1721
01:19:34.200 --> 01:19:37.170
I could either still be with her and still be a child,

1722
01:19:37.170 --> 01:19:40.860
and be loved or I could grow up and develop and be myself,

1723
01:19:40.860 --> 01:19:41.883
and be cast out.

1724
01:19:43.980 --> 01:19:46.620
And I don't think I ever really questioned the choice

1725
01:19:46.620 --> 01:19:50.403
so I went and moved in a separate direction.

1726
01:19:52.170 --> 01:19:55.950
But the casting out part was like incredibly,

1727
01:19:55.950 --> 01:19:57.393
incredibly painful,

1728
01:19:58.710 --> 01:20:01.170
and my way of dealing with it because I'm a writer,

1729
01:20:01.170 --> 01:20:03.903
and I'd loved writing since I was four years old,

1730
01:20:04.770 --> 01:20:08.340
I wrote everything down into these diaries that I kept,

1731
01:20:08.340 --> 01:20:10.980
and went off to college.

1732
01:20:10.980 --> 01:20:15.750
And at the end of freshman year, for some reason,

1733
01:20:15.750 --> 01:20:18.180
I had to stay on campus for a few extra days

1734
01:20:18.180 --> 01:20:20.400
and my parents came to take my stuff home.

1735
01:20:20.400 --> 01:20:23.160
And just as they were getting ready to leave,

1736
01:20:23.160 --> 01:20:25.650
and they're like leaving with all my suitcases,

1737
01:20:25.650 --> 01:20:29.313
I take my diaries and I hand them to my mother.

1738
01:20:30.900 --> 01:20:32.040
And I thought at the time,

1739
01:20:32.040 --> 01:20:33.930
like I truly on a conscious level,

1740
01:20:33.930 --> 01:20:37.200
I thought I was just giving them to her for safe keeping,

1741
01:20:37.200 --> 01:20:39.500
I didn't have a place to store them at school.

1742
01:20:41.010 --> 01:20:42.570
And I never thought that she would read them,

1743
01:20:42.570 --> 01:20:44.190
on a conscious level,

1744
01:20:44.190 --> 01:20:48.717
but of course she did and whatever I had written there,

1745
01:20:48.717 --> 01:20:52.050
and I say whatever, 'cause I've never read them again since,

1746
01:20:52.050 --> 01:20:53.940
but whatever I had written there,

1747
01:20:53.940 --> 01:20:58.500
kind of like put the final nail in the coffin

1748
01:20:58.500 --> 01:21:00.120
of our relationship.

1749
01:21:00.120 --> 01:21:03.720
And so for the decades after,

1750
01:21:03.720 --> 01:21:06.150
we were still seeing each other at holidays

1751
01:21:06.150 --> 01:21:08.610
and doing all the regular family stuff,

1752
01:21:08.610 --> 01:21:13.110
but the relationship really was never the same again,

1753
01:21:13.110 --> 01:21:14.537
and it was like,

1754
01:21:14.537 --> 01:21:16.533
it was a loss for me that,

1755
01:21:19.170 --> 01:21:21.210
for years I couldn't even talk about

1756
01:21:21.210 --> 01:21:23.910
my mother without crying, I couldn't mention her name.

1757
01:21:25.620 --> 01:21:30.330
But I will say there's been this amazing rap posh mom.

1758
01:21:30.330 --> 01:21:33.753
Now my mother is Alzheimer's and,

1759
01:21:36.450 --> 01:21:39.450
she only has a few conversational lanes that she can travel,

1760
01:21:39.450 --> 01:21:42.750
but when she's on those lanes, she still feels like herself.

1761
01:21:42.750 --> 01:21:46.410
And she has completely forgotten all the difficult years

1762
01:21:46.410 --> 01:21:48.750
we had, she's forgotten them.

1763
01:21:48.750 --> 01:21:52.710
And so all this time through my adulthood,

1764
01:21:52.710 --> 01:21:54.840
I had always been asking myself,

1765
01:21:54.840 --> 01:21:58.260
that childhood that you remember as being so wonderful,

1766
01:21:58.260 --> 01:21:59.700
like maybe it wasn't really true,

1767
01:21:59.700 --> 01:22:03.300
maybe that was just like a child's misperception,

1768
01:22:03.300 --> 01:22:07.800
but now the mother of that childhood has come back.

1769
01:22:07.800 --> 01:22:09.040
And I realize like

1770
01:22:14.279 --> 01:22:16.260
we still are that way,

1771
01:22:16.260 --> 01:22:19.890
the relationship we have now is the same relationship we had

1772
01:22:19.890 --> 01:22:20.793
when I was a kid.

1773
01:22:22.020 --> 01:22:24.693
So that's been a kind of amazing.

1774
01:22:25.920 --> 01:22:27.447
Such an interesting story,

1775
01:22:27.447 --> 01:22:30.570
and certainly there's bitter sweetness in it, of course.

1776
01:22:30.570 --> 01:22:31.403
Oh yeah, yeah.

1777
01:22:31.403 --> 01:22:32.850
So much.

1778
01:22:32.850 --> 01:22:35.610
But the idea that you were faced with this choice

1779
01:22:35.610 --> 01:22:38.760
of either reverting into a childlike state,

1780
01:22:38.760 --> 01:22:41.670
or just becoming the person that

1781
01:22:41.670 --> 01:22:43.740
your mother actually wanted you to be,

1782
01:22:43.740 --> 01:22:46.920
put you in an impossible situation,

1783
01:22:46.920 --> 01:22:49.770
without her being consciously aware of

1784
01:22:49.770 --> 01:22:54.360
the kind of psychic toll that that would place on you.

1785
01:22:54.360 --> 01:22:55.950
And then the bittersweetness

1786
01:22:55.950 --> 01:23:00.023
of never really fully reconciling that later, right?

1787
01:23:00.023 --> 01:23:02.700
I mean, I read it as somebody who,

1788
01:23:02.700 --> 01:23:05.010
because of her past traumas and pain

1789
01:23:05.010 --> 01:23:07.830
that she hadn't adequately worked through,

1790
01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:10.410
there's this transference, like she wants you to be safe.

1791
01:23:10.410 --> 01:23:14.010
She wants you to be upwardly mobile and all of these things.

1792
01:23:14.010 --> 01:23:16.660
And she lovingly drives you

1793
01:23:17.550 --> 01:23:19.950
on this kind of ambitious journey

1794
01:23:19.950 --> 01:23:21.840
to achieving all these amazing things.

1795
01:23:21.840 --> 01:23:25.350
But as you achieve them, of course, separation occurs.

1796
01:23:25.350 --> 01:23:29.070
And that's a threat to this bond that she has,

1797
01:23:29.070 --> 01:23:30.750
that is so meaningful.

1798
01:23:30.750 --> 01:23:35.750
For her, that is in part driven by the pain of her past

1799
01:23:36.120 --> 01:23:39.930
that creates this unhealthy soup where she's transferring

1800
01:23:39.930 --> 01:23:42.270
so much of her identity onto you,

1801
01:23:42.270 --> 01:23:45.513
and you're as a young person having to shoulder that burden.

1802
01:23:46.740 --> 01:23:48.243
Yeah, yeah.

1803
01:23:51.210 --> 01:23:53.400
Yeah, it's funny I'm actually gonna

1804
01:23:53.400 --> 01:23:54.840
amend something I said before,

1805
01:23:54.840 --> 01:23:58.500
because I think I said a minute ago that,

1806
01:23:58.500 --> 01:24:01.020
I had never really considered doing anything

1807
01:24:01.020 --> 01:24:03.330
other than growing into a separate person,

1808
01:24:03.330 --> 01:24:05.520
but actually I'm not sure that's quite right,

1809
01:24:05.520 --> 01:24:08.460
because I do remember many times saying to myself,

1810
01:24:08.460 --> 01:24:10.830
like I loved her so much.

1811
01:24:10.830 --> 01:24:12.600
All I wanted was for her to be happy.

1812
01:24:12.600 --> 01:24:15.750
So I felt like, well, maybe I should do some of these things

1813
01:24:15.750 --> 01:24:19.470
that she's asking, so that she can still be happy,

1814
01:24:19.470 --> 01:24:20.820
which is a very difficult thing.

1815
01:24:20.820 --> 01:24:23.090
Right, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

1816
01:24:23.090 --> 01:24:25.110
And did you have a sense of her self-awareness

1817
01:24:25.110 --> 01:24:27.570
around any of this or just, no?

1818
01:24:27.570 --> 01:24:29.610
I don't think so.

1819
01:24:29.610 --> 01:24:31.950
Yeah, I don't really know.

1820
01:24:31.950 --> 01:24:34.320
I don't think there was so much of it,

1821
01:24:34.320 --> 01:24:38.310
but what I will say happened, excuse me, over the years.

1822
01:24:38.310 --> 01:24:40.650
And especially during the time I wrote this book,

1823
01:24:40.650 --> 01:24:43.770
as I told you, it used to be that I couldn't

1824
01:24:43.770 --> 01:24:46.197
talk about her at all without crying.

1825
01:24:46.197 --> 01:24:49.293
And I wrote her story, I wrote the story in the book,

1826
01:24:51.150 --> 01:24:53.490
and I was like really worried about what was gonna happen

1827
01:24:53.490 --> 01:24:57.390
when I would go out and do interviews about the book.

1828
01:24:57.390 --> 01:24:59.430
I thought, I won't be able to speak about this

1829
01:24:59.430 --> 01:25:01.200
without breaking down.

1830
01:25:01.200 --> 01:25:02.790
And despite everything I'm saying in the book

1831
01:25:02.790 --> 01:25:05.220
about being comfortable with all our emotions,

1832
01:25:05.220 --> 01:25:07.083
I didn't really wanna do that.

1833
01:25:09.000 --> 01:25:10.817
So I didn't know what would happen (laughs).

1834
01:25:14.016 --> 01:25:17.430
But I remember talking to somebody about this dilemma,

1835
01:25:17.430 --> 01:25:19.350
and he said to me,

1836
01:25:19.350 --> 01:25:20.730
talk to me when you're done writing the book,

1837
01:25:20.730 --> 01:25:24.030
because you might actually resolve some of these issues.

1838
01:25:24.030 --> 01:25:25.950
And at the time he said that, I thought, oh my gosh,

1839
01:25:25.950 --> 01:25:27.250
that's never gonna happen.

1840
01:25:28.710 --> 01:25:30.993
But in fact, that's exactly what happened.

1841
01:25:31.860 --> 01:25:34.600
There's just a sense of peace

1842
01:25:35.520 --> 01:25:38.973
that I now have with everything that happened.

1843
01:25:40.380 --> 01:25:42.120
I don't know, just so much love for her

1844
01:25:42.120 --> 01:25:44.940
and understanding of it all and,

1845
01:25:44.940 --> 01:25:48.510
like, wishing that it had been different, but excuse me,

1846
01:25:48.510 --> 01:25:52.230
but I don't know, that's just a complete reconciliation.

1847
01:25:52.230 --> 01:25:54.600
Well, that's the beauty and the gift

1848
01:25:54.600 --> 01:25:57.000
of reckoning with your bitter sweetness, right?

1849
01:25:57.000 --> 01:26:00.420
Like you can have some kinship with Leonard Cohen,

1850
01:26:00.420 --> 01:26:03.303
who I'm sure similarly was having,

1851
01:26:04.170 --> 01:26:07.230
conflicting emotions and was in some sort of pain

1852
01:26:07.230 --> 01:26:08.580
and he was trying to work through it.

1853
01:26:08.580 --> 01:26:09.413
So what does he do?

1854
01:26:09.413 --> 01:26:10.680
He writes a song about it, right?

1855
01:26:10.680 --> 01:26:12.210
You write a book about it,

1856
01:26:12.210 --> 01:26:14.610
and in the process of writing this book,

1857
01:26:14.610 --> 01:26:18.390
you find a way to heal and make peace with yourself,

1858
01:26:18.390 --> 01:26:21.270
and your loved ones and come out the other side,

1859
01:26:21.270 --> 01:26:23.190
like a more complete human.

1860
01:26:23.190 --> 01:26:25.350
And that goes to the very thesis of the book,

1861
01:26:25.350 --> 01:26:28.830
which is channeling your pain and

1862
01:26:28.830 --> 01:26:30.630
turning it into a creative offering.

1863
01:26:30.630 --> 01:26:34.680
So it's like a meta exam.

1864
01:26:34.680 --> 01:26:36.810
Like it's all very meta in that regard, right?

1865
01:26:36.810 --> 01:26:37.770
Yeah.

1866
01:26:37.770 --> 01:26:39.093
Yeah, I know, that's true.

1867
01:26:40.506 --> 01:26:41.937
And I do wanna be careful to say like,

1868
01:26:41.937 --> 01:26:44.190
for anybody listening,

1869
01:26:44.190 --> 01:26:46.170
again, that we use that word creative

1870
01:26:46.170 --> 01:26:47.400
in a really general way.

1871
01:26:47.400 --> 01:26:49.530
So it's not like you have to go and write a book

1872
01:26:49.530 --> 01:26:52.740
or write a song or like be a Grammy award winner,

1873
01:26:52.740 --> 01:26:54.810
or something, it has nothing to do with that.

1874
01:26:54.810 --> 01:26:58.320
It's more just about going through a kind of transformation

1875
01:26:58.320 --> 01:27:01.500
of like taking the pain and transforming it

1876
01:27:01.500 --> 01:27:02.790
into something of meaning.

1877
01:27:02.790 --> 01:27:06.570
Yeah, well just confronting it and walking with it,

1878
01:27:06.570 --> 01:27:09.750
and finding a way to come to the other side

1879
01:27:09.750 --> 01:27:14.190
through whatever, modality suits you.

1880
01:27:14.190 --> 01:27:15.210
Yeah, and it's funny,

1881
01:27:15.210 --> 01:27:17.940
I've been trying to write this story for so many years.

1882
01:27:17.940 --> 01:27:19.170
Like I said this in the book,

1883
01:27:19.170 --> 01:27:22.380
I first started writing about it when I was in college,

1884
01:27:22.380 --> 01:27:24.427
and my creative writing teacher said,

1885
01:27:24.427 --> 01:27:25.987
"You're way too close to this story.

1886
01:27:25.987 --> 01:27:27.367
"You should like put it in a drawer

1887
01:27:27.367 --> 01:27:29.757
"and not take it out again for 30 years."

1888
01:27:30.930 --> 01:27:33.618
So it's been a long time, and now it's 30 years later, yeah.

1889
01:27:33.618 --> 01:27:34.451
You're too close to it.

1890
01:27:34.451 --> 01:27:36.393
But you're still so close to it,

1891
01:27:37.844 --> 01:27:40.290
but you're distant enough from it

1892
01:27:40.290 --> 01:27:42.390
that you could write about it

1893
01:27:42.390 --> 01:27:44.610
with some level of objectivity,

1894
01:27:44.610 --> 01:27:49.050
but the fact that it creates so much emotion in you,

1895
01:27:49.050 --> 01:27:51.270
it's still very present with you.

1896
01:27:51.270 --> 01:27:53.620
Yeah, it's still very present, but like

1897
01:27:55.950 --> 01:27:59.130
with a lot less turmoil than there used to be.

1898
01:27:59.130 --> 01:28:02.190
I experience very little turmoil now talking about it

1899
01:28:02.190 --> 01:28:06.090
because I really do feel such a sense of resolution

1900
01:28:06.090 --> 01:28:09.660
between us and between like my relationship to my mother

1901
01:28:09.660 --> 01:28:11.660
in my heart, is very different.

1902
01:28:11.660 --> 01:28:12.493
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1903
01:28:12.493 --> 01:28:13.326
From what it had been.

1904
01:28:13.326 --> 01:28:14.159
Yeah, beautiful.

1905
01:28:15.450 --> 01:28:20.450
On this idea of binaries,

1906
01:28:21.360 --> 01:28:26.360
heaven, hell, rich, poor, and the like,

1907
01:28:26.430 --> 01:28:30.385
there is this undercurrent in our society

1908
01:28:30.385 --> 01:28:32.820
around the pursuit of happiness, right?

1909
01:28:32.820 --> 01:28:34.680
It's in the declaration of independence,

1910
01:28:34.680 --> 01:28:36.240
like the pursuit of happiness.

1911
01:28:36.240 --> 01:28:39.063
And now we're in a happiness obsessed culture.

1912
01:28:39.990 --> 01:28:42.283
I think we're getting to a point where

1913
01:28:42.283 --> 01:28:45.450
we're maturely grappling with what happiness means

1914
01:28:45.450 --> 01:28:47.340
and trying to create a healthier relationship

1915
01:28:47.340 --> 01:28:49.590
with how to build that into our lives.

1916
01:28:49.590 --> 01:28:53.730
But in reading your book, I couldn't help but think,

1917
01:28:53.730 --> 01:28:57.690
what would happen if that phrase

1918
01:28:57.690 --> 01:29:00.440
in the declaration of independence said something like,

1919
01:29:02.070 --> 01:29:06.630
may you pursue longing or something like that.

1920
01:29:06.630 --> 01:29:09.120
May you grapple with your own bittersweetness.

1921
01:29:09.120 --> 01:29:12.120
Like if we had created a cultural priority

1922
01:29:12.120 --> 01:29:14.790
around a more nuanced relationship

1923
01:29:14.790 --> 01:29:16.890
with the conflicting emotions

1924
01:29:16.890 --> 01:29:18.900
about what it means to be human,

1925
01:29:18.900 --> 01:29:20.790
how would that percolate into our culture

1926
01:29:20.790 --> 01:29:25.410
and change our relationship with the darker side of things

1927
01:29:25.410 --> 01:29:28.920
that make the brighter things so much brighter?

1928
01:29:28.920 --> 01:29:30.420
Yeah, it's an interesting question.

1929
01:29:30.420 --> 01:29:31.320
I've thought about that

1930
01:29:31.320 --> 01:29:33.413
with the declaration of independence.

1931
01:29:33.413 --> 01:29:36.903
I mean, I enjoy being happy as much as the next person,

1932
01:29:38.512 --> 01:29:40.650
so I don't think happiness is the problem,

1933
01:29:40.650 --> 01:29:42.720
but maybe it's just how we define happy.

1934
01:29:42.720 --> 01:29:43.740
Maybe meaning would've been-

1935
01:29:43.740 --> 01:29:45.570
Happiness at the cost of everything else.

1936
01:29:45.570 --> 01:29:49.080
Yeah, and I think we end up interpreting happiness

1937
01:29:49.080 --> 01:29:51.840
as a kind of like hedonic type of happiness,

1938
01:29:51.840 --> 01:29:54.210
as opposed to a happiness where you feel

1939
01:29:54.210 --> 01:29:56.493
a deep sense of meaning in your life.

1940
01:29:58.650 --> 01:30:02.760
And I also think part of the problem is that,

1941
01:30:02.760 --> 01:30:04.950
we have such a separation of church and state,

1942
01:30:04.950 --> 01:30:06.720
which is great in most ways,

1943
01:30:06.720 --> 01:30:09.990
but it means that all these questions

1944
01:30:09.990 --> 01:30:13.560
that religion naturally grapples with,

1945
01:30:13.560 --> 01:30:17.670
like this existential longing that we all have,

1946
01:30:17.670 --> 01:30:20.790
we tend to relegate that to what you experience,

1947
01:30:20.790 --> 01:30:22.350
at church on Sundays,

1948
01:30:22.350 --> 01:30:24.660
as opposed to just thinking of that

1949
01:30:24.660 --> 01:30:26.043
as part of everyday life.

1950
01:30:26.940 --> 01:30:27.773
Hmm.

1951
01:30:29.550 --> 01:30:34.550
Yeah, but as we become more agnostic as a culture,

1952
01:30:37.170 --> 01:30:39.610
are we not adequately

1953
01:30:41.070 --> 01:30:44.760
in constructs where we are actively confronted with

1954
01:30:44.760 --> 01:30:48.660
grappling with these philosophical questions and-

1955
01:30:48.660 --> 01:30:50.040
Wait, say the question again.

1956
01:30:50.040 --> 01:30:50.920
Meaning, like

1957
01:30:52.980 --> 01:30:56.190
you're saying that the purview of that kind of inquiry

1958
01:30:56.190 --> 01:30:59.313
would be in the church or whatever spiritual tradition,

1959
01:31:01.320 --> 01:31:04.080
we've become a culture that's that seems at least,

1960
01:31:04.080 --> 01:31:08.730
from my perspective, like less interested

1961
01:31:08.730 --> 01:31:12.100
in those types of outlets.

1962
01:31:14.376 --> 01:31:17.250
And so where does that leave us in terms of,

1963
01:31:17.250 --> 01:31:19.590
how we're dealing with those bigger spiritual

1964
01:31:19.590 --> 01:31:21.030
and philosophical questions?

1965
01:31:21.030 --> 01:31:22.140
Yeah, that's the problem,

1966
01:31:22.140 --> 01:31:24.570
'cause we've always relegated it just to that.

1967
01:31:24.570 --> 01:31:26.261
So it was already bad enough when we relegated-

1968
01:31:26.261 --> 01:31:27.094
But we're not going there anymore.

1969
01:31:27.094 --> 01:31:28.320
But now we don't even go there.

1970
01:31:28.320 --> 01:31:29.153
So now it's kind of nowhere.

1971
01:31:29.153 --> 01:31:31.953
Because dancing with the stars is on and, you know?

1972
01:31:33.120 --> 01:31:34.083
Yeah, exactly.

1973
01:31:37.470 --> 01:31:41.010
I think we need to find ways of introducing this

1974
01:31:41.010 --> 01:31:42.630
into part of everyday discourse,

1975
01:31:42.630 --> 01:31:46.260
and you could see how much we long for it because

1976
01:31:46.260 --> 01:31:47.093
you look at,

1977
01:31:48.000 --> 01:31:51.000
to take my patron Saint of Leonard Cohen,

1978
01:31:51.000 --> 01:31:53.250
like there's a reason that his song "Hallelujah,"

1979
01:31:53.250 --> 01:31:56.730
was making people cry for years on end.

1980
01:31:56.730 --> 01:31:58.770
It's like the most covered song I think in history

1981
01:31:58.770 --> 01:32:00.720
or something like that.

1982
01:32:00.720 --> 01:32:02.070
People are relating to that.

1983
01:32:02.070 --> 01:32:05.070
As soon as they hear it, they open up to it.

1984
01:32:05.070 --> 01:32:08.400
So we need to be finding different ways of introducing this

1985
01:32:08.400 --> 01:32:09.783
into everyday life.

1986
01:32:10.890 --> 01:32:13.290
And I think part of that would actually be just,

1987
01:32:14.370 --> 01:32:18.570
proactively engaging with beauty more in everyday life,

1988
01:32:18.570 --> 01:32:21.510
like at work at school,

1989
01:32:21.510 --> 01:32:25.920
like actively encouraging people to bring in

1990
01:32:25.920 --> 01:32:28.200
things that they find beautiful or meaningful,

1991
01:32:28.200 --> 01:32:29.520
and be sharing those.

1992
01:32:29.520 --> 01:32:31.710
You don't even have to talk about bittersweetness so much

1993
01:32:31.710 --> 01:32:34.140
it's like through engaging with beauty,

1994
01:32:34.140 --> 01:32:35.253
all of that comes out.

1995
01:32:36.720 --> 01:32:37.553
How do you feel about

1996
01:32:37.553 --> 01:32:39.550
Jeff Buckley's rendition of "Hallelujah?"

1997
01:32:40.512 --> 01:32:41.550
Is that sacrilege?

1998
01:32:41.550 --> 01:32:43.380
It's not sacrilege at all.

1999
01:32:43.380 --> 01:32:44.213
I like it.

2000
01:32:44.213 --> 01:32:46.743
It's not one of my favorites, but I do really like it.

2001
01:32:47.970 --> 01:32:49.590
But no, I'm all for covers, it's okay.

2002
01:32:49.590 --> 01:32:51.600
He captures the bitter sweetness.

2003
01:32:51.600 --> 01:32:52.433
He sure does.

2004
01:32:52.433 --> 01:32:53.340
In his music, beautifully.

2005
01:32:53.340 --> 01:32:54.780
He really does.

2006
01:32:54.780 --> 01:32:57.270
I like the Rufus Wayne Wright one.

2007
01:32:57.270 --> 01:32:59.070
Oh yes, very good.

2008
01:32:59.070 --> 01:32:59.903
Yeah.

2009
01:32:59.903 --> 01:33:01.380
Sorry, I've forgotten about that one.

2010
01:33:03.690 --> 01:33:07.620
I think a good story to tell would be the story of Beethoven

2011
01:33:07.620 --> 01:33:11.040
that you share around that performance,

2012
01:33:11.040 --> 01:33:15.393
because that so kind of captures this idea of,

2013
01:33:18.150 --> 01:33:21.900
channeling that emotional experience into something shared.

2014
01:33:21.900 --> 01:33:23.100
Yeah.

2015
01:33:23.100 --> 01:33:26.700
That breeds that community piece, right?

2016
01:33:26.700 --> 01:33:29.520
Yeah, so Beethoven, one of his,

2017
01:33:29.520 --> 01:33:32.430
maybe his most famous work is "Ode to Joy,"

2018
01:33:32.430 --> 01:33:33.660
which everybody loves so much.

2019
01:33:33.660 --> 01:33:36.273
Maybe we could even play it somehow into the episode.

2020
01:33:36.273 --> 01:33:37.244
I'll drop it in.

2021
01:33:37.244 --> 01:33:39.450
Yeah, drop it in.

2022
01:33:39.450 --> 01:33:43.860
And "Ode to Joy" actually started out as a poem

2023
01:33:43.860 --> 01:33:45.183
by Frederick Schiller,

2024
01:33:46.650 --> 01:33:49.053
and Beethoven loved this poem because,

2025
01:33:50.070 --> 01:33:53.280
it expressed like all these ideals of love and brotherhood

2026
01:33:53.280 --> 01:33:54.690
that were really important to him.

2027
01:33:54.690 --> 01:33:56.640
So he labored for years,

2028
01:33:56.640 --> 01:33:58.620
I wanna say it was 30 years, maybe getting that wrong,

2029
01:33:58.620 --> 01:34:02.610
but like forever, to set this poem to music.

2030
01:34:02.610 --> 01:34:04.620
But during the time that he's working on it,

2031
01:34:04.620 --> 01:34:06.750
he starts to lose his hearing.

2032
01:34:06.750 --> 01:34:09.333
And he has all these other personal troubles.

2033
01:34:12.987 --> 01:34:15.060
By the time of the debut performance,

2034
01:34:15.060 --> 01:34:19.080
after all these years of creating "Ode to Joy,"

2035
01:34:19.080 --> 01:34:21.390
he's now totally deaf.

2036
01:34:21.390 --> 01:34:25.980
And yet he's like desperate for this music to be performed

2037
01:34:25.980 --> 01:34:28.350
the way he hears it in his head.

2038
01:34:28.350 --> 01:34:32.100
And so he stands on stage with the orchestra,

2039
01:34:32.100 --> 01:34:33.930
with his back turned to the audience,

2040
01:34:33.930 --> 01:34:35.280
and he's facing the orchestra.

2041
01:34:35.280 --> 01:34:37.230
And he's like throwing his body around

2042
01:34:37.230 --> 01:34:39.870
trying to direct them to play the music

2043
01:34:39.870 --> 01:34:41.490
the way he sees it in his mind.

2044
01:34:41.490 --> 01:34:43.290
He's standing next to the conductor.

2045
01:34:46.011 --> 01:34:48.570
(orchestral music)

2046
01:34:48.570 --> 01:34:49.590
Because he can't hear any of it,

2047
01:34:49.590 --> 01:34:52.020
he doesn't even realize when the music is actually over,

2048
01:34:52.020 --> 01:34:53.790
when they're done playing.

2049
01:34:53.790 --> 01:34:57.990
But when it's finished, there's a 20 year old soprano.

2050
01:34:57.990 --> 01:35:01.170
And she turns him around to face the audience,

2051
01:35:01.170 --> 01:35:02.520
who are so overwhelmed.

2052
01:35:02.520 --> 01:35:04.440
They're like standing up in their seats

2053
01:35:04.440 --> 01:35:06.480
with tears streaming down their faces,

2054
01:35:06.480 --> 01:35:09.660
and raising their hands and tribute to this man

2055
01:35:09.660 --> 01:35:12.600
who's given them this experience of longing,

2056
01:35:12.600 --> 01:35:16.110
which you listen to that music and it's an "Ode to Joy."

2057
01:35:16.110 --> 01:35:17.940
It's literally about joy.

2058
01:35:17.940 --> 01:35:21.930
And yet you can't help but hear the longing and the sorrow

2059
01:35:21.930 --> 01:35:23.700
that's like echoing in its notes.

2060
01:35:23.700 --> 01:35:25.080
It's just the most incredible,

2061
01:35:25.080 --> 01:35:27.960
that's what makes the music so incredible.

2062
01:35:27.960 --> 01:35:29.760
And in fact,

2063
01:35:29.760 --> 01:35:33.240
there is an economist at MIT,

2064
01:35:33.240 --> 01:35:34.590
whose name I'm forgetting right now,

2065
01:35:34.590 --> 01:35:37.020
but he did this fascinating study where,

2066
01:35:37.020 --> 01:35:41.880
he looked at Mozart Beethoven and list at their music.

2067
01:35:41.880 --> 01:35:44.430
And he read all the different letters

2068
01:35:44.430 --> 01:35:46.170
they had written over their lifetimes

2069
01:35:46.170 --> 01:35:49.170
and he coded the letters for emotions.

2070
01:35:49.170 --> 01:35:51.783
And he found that during the periods

2071
01:35:51.783 --> 01:35:54.630
when their letters had a lot of emotions expressed,

2072
01:35:54.630 --> 01:35:57.030
sorrow and longing and grief, and like that,

2073
01:35:57.030 --> 01:35:58.560
that was when they were composing

2074
01:35:58.560 --> 01:36:01.050
their most profound best works of art.

2075
01:36:01.050 --> 01:36:01.883
Wow.

2076
01:36:01.883 --> 01:36:03.240
Yeah, yeah.

2077
01:36:03.240 --> 01:36:05.337
There's just this incredible confluence there.

2078
01:36:05.337 --> 01:36:07.680
And I feel like we know it instinctively.

2079
01:36:07.680 --> 01:36:08.940
Yeah.

2080
01:36:08.940 --> 01:36:10.940
But we're not sure what to do with it.

2081
01:36:12.065 --> 01:36:12.898
And we don't wanna go deaf-

2082
01:36:12.898 --> 01:36:15.000
It's hard to like canonize it, like,

2083
01:36:15.000 --> 01:36:18.360
oh, this note and this note, it's more than that, right?

2084
01:36:18.360 --> 01:36:20.940
Like it's the performance of the music as well.

2085
01:36:20.940 --> 01:36:23.550
You have the story of the violinist, right?

2086
01:36:23.550 --> 01:36:26.970
Like when you are carrying a certain emotional resonance,

2087
01:36:26.970 --> 01:36:30.690
it gets translated into those notes in a certain way

2088
01:36:30.690 --> 01:36:34.650
that we immediately recognize, but you can't quite define.

2089
01:36:34.650 --> 01:36:36.450
And in telling that story about Beethoven,

2090
01:36:36.450 --> 01:36:37.350
it's very cinematic.

2091
01:36:37.350 --> 01:36:39.513
Like I imagine like, he's just,

2092
01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:44.670
like doing his thing with such dramatic physicality,

2093
01:36:44.670 --> 01:36:47.550
but because he can't hear he's completely detached

2094
01:36:47.550 --> 01:36:51.120
with how it's being received and perhaps even thinking like,

2095
01:36:51.120 --> 01:36:52.470
is this even working at all?

2096
01:36:52.470 --> 01:36:56.610
Or am I able to kind of get out of these musicians,

2097
01:36:56.610 --> 01:36:58.470
the performance that I hear in my mind,

2098
01:36:58.470 --> 01:37:02.370
only to find out that it is United and really just,

2099
01:37:02.370 --> 01:37:05.487
uplifted and connected this huge group of people.

2100
01:37:05.487 --> 01:37:08.880
And I think it's really profound example of

2101
01:37:08.880 --> 01:37:13.880
the power of sharing those types of emotions

2102
01:37:14.310 --> 01:37:18.510
and figuring out a way to create a container for it

2103
01:37:18.510 --> 01:37:20.400
that people can consume.

2104
01:37:20.400 --> 01:37:24.810
That really is this antidote to what is dividing us.

2105
01:37:24.810 --> 01:37:26.400
And it's like, I don't have to tell you,

2106
01:37:26.400 --> 01:37:28.500
it does feel so divided out there.

2107
01:37:28.500 --> 01:37:31.620
And I often despair of our ability to kind of

2108
01:37:31.620 --> 01:37:33.060
mend that division.

2109
01:37:33.060 --> 01:37:36.150
And it used to be when a tragic of event befell

2110
01:37:36.150 --> 01:37:38.730
the country or the world, we would feel like one,

2111
01:37:38.730 --> 01:37:40.770
we would feel like that's super organism.

2112
01:37:40.770 --> 01:37:43.710
And now it feels like when that happens,

2113
01:37:43.710 --> 01:37:46.410
everybody retreats to their corner and figures out

2114
01:37:46.410 --> 01:37:48.120
how to blame the other side for it.

2115
01:37:48.120 --> 01:37:50.460
And like, how do we move forward

2116
01:37:50.460 --> 01:37:52.740
as a functional, healthy society

2117
01:37:52.740 --> 01:37:56.460
with this sort of rift and dynamic

2118
01:37:56.460 --> 01:37:59.193
that doesn't seem to be going away anytime soon.

2119
01:38:00.120 --> 01:38:01.170
Yeah, I know.

2120
01:38:01.170 --> 01:38:02.850
And I mean, I don't have answers

2121
01:38:02.850 --> 01:38:05.790
anymore than anybody has the answer,

2122
01:38:05.790 --> 01:38:07.410
but I do think that one thing that

2123
01:38:07.410 --> 01:38:10.590
we could and should be doing is creating spaces for people

2124
01:38:10.590 --> 01:38:13.263
to truly be telling their stories,

2125
01:38:14.790 --> 01:38:17.220
but to have the stories not be attached

2126
01:38:17.220 --> 01:38:20.610
to policy prescriptions, or politics or anything like that,

2127
01:38:20.610 --> 01:38:22.770
just tell each other the stories,

2128
01:38:22.770 --> 01:38:25.713
just get to know each other on that elemental level.

2129
01:38:27.750 --> 01:38:30.000
Obviously like our social media platforms right now

2130
01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:32.430
are designed to do the exact opposite of that.

2131
01:38:32.430 --> 01:38:35.700
But sometimes like if we could have a social media platform

2132
01:38:35.700 --> 01:38:40.700
that would give people space to just share it all,

2133
01:38:41.250 --> 01:38:43.216
maybe it needs to be done anonymously.

2134
01:38:43.216 --> 01:38:44.049
Or something like that.

2135
01:38:44.049 --> 01:38:45.160
Yeah, something like that.

2136
01:38:45.160 --> 01:38:46.239
Yeah, it is...

2137
01:38:46.239 --> 01:38:47.072
Sorry, go ahead.

2138
01:38:47.072 --> 01:38:47.905
No, I was just gonna say,

2139
01:38:47.905 --> 01:38:50.280
not everybody wants to tell their story with a name attached

2140
01:38:50.280 --> 01:38:52.800
the way you do with a moth, but you know how-

2141
01:38:52.800 --> 01:38:54.390
Maybe there's a way to anonymize it.

2142
01:38:54.390 --> 01:38:55.770
Yeah, yeah.

2143
01:38:55.770 --> 01:38:57.270
Or have that option.

2144
01:38:57.270 --> 01:38:58.230
Yeah.

2145
01:38:58.230 --> 01:39:02.070
I think there's something about storytelling,

2146
01:39:02.070 --> 01:39:04.890
like honest, open, vulnerable storytelling

2147
01:39:04.890 --> 01:39:08.460
that is akin to this notion of bittersweetness,

2148
01:39:08.460 --> 01:39:11.100
and perhaps it emanates from the Vagus nerve as well.

2149
01:39:11.100 --> 01:39:14.590
Like when we hear somebody share a story

2150
01:39:16.290 --> 01:39:20.100
and we have the sense that that story is true to that person

2151
01:39:20.100 --> 01:39:23.250
and honest and authentically rendered,

2152
01:39:23.250 --> 01:39:25.260
that also breeds connection, right?

2153
01:39:25.260 --> 01:39:27.840
Like whether it's bittersweet or not like just,

2154
01:39:27.840 --> 01:39:31.470
the storytelling as a vehicle for connection,

2155
01:39:31.470 --> 01:39:36.180
and also learning, like it doesn't have to be prescriptive,

2156
01:39:36.180 --> 01:39:38.700
but it's more effective than a prescription

2157
01:39:38.700 --> 01:39:42.960
when whatever that notion is kind of wet into the narrative.

2158
01:39:42.960 --> 01:39:46.980
Like we, as human beings are able to kind of synthesize that

2159
01:39:46.980 --> 01:39:50.070
and then ultimately kind of hold onto it

2160
01:39:50.070 --> 01:39:53.490
and perhaps even practice it in our lives in a way that

2161
01:39:53.490 --> 01:39:54.990
is very different than if somebody said,

2162
01:39:54.990 --> 01:39:57.600
here's the five things that you need to do today.

2163
01:39:57.600 --> 01:40:01.173
We remember this story, we forget the listical.

2164
01:40:02.160 --> 01:40:04.080
And I think there's something really profound about

2165
01:40:04.080 --> 01:40:06.420
that as well that perhaps,

2166
01:40:06.420 --> 01:40:08.850
can be traced back evolutionarily,

2167
01:40:08.850 --> 01:40:13.850
back to the campfires of your, and the like.

2168
01:40:15.180 --> 01:40:16.013
Absolutely, I mean,

2169
01:40:16.013 --> 01:40:19.200
I actually think podcasts play a huge role in that.

2170
01:40:19.200 --> 01:40:21.300
I think part of the reason that podcasts have become

2171
01:40:21.300 --> 01:40:23.860
the amazing medium that they are is that,

2172
01:40:25.034 --> 01:40:28.020
they're touching into that campfire impulse that we have.

2173
01:40:28.020 --> 01:40:29.880
There's something about the voice in your ear

2174
01:40:29.880 --> 01:40:31.890
as you're listening, where you feel like

2175
01:40:31.890 --> 01:40:33.990
you're like bonded with all the other listeners

2176
01:40:33.990 --> 01:40:36.000
sitting around a campfire, it's connection.

2177
01:40:36.000 --> 01:40:37.590
Exactly, exactly.

2178
01:40:37.590 --> 01:40:39.720
Yeah, so there you go.

2179
01:40:39.720 --> 01:40:40.864
There's your next book.

2180
01:40:40.864 --> 01:40:41.880
(Susan laughs)

2181
01:40:41.880 --> 01:40:43.350
What are you working on now?

2182
01:40:43.350 --> 01:40:46.560
Are you on another seven year Jat to the next one?

2183
01:40:46.560 --> 01:40:49.230
Well, I mean, I probably will be at some point,

2184
01:40:49.230 --> 01:40:51.570
and I have two different book ideas that I'm playing with,

2185
01:40:51.570 --> 01:40:54.090
but I actually wanna start a podcast also.

2186
01:40:54.090 --> 01:40:56.610
So I'm probably gonna be doing that in the next year.

2187
01:40:56.610 --> 01:40:58.500
And that's gonna be my next big project.

2188
01:40:58.500 --> 01:40:59.550
Oh, that's exciting.

2189
01:40:59.550 --> 01:41:00.540
Can you say more about that

2190
01:41:00.540 --> 01:41:01.860
or is that under wraps right now?

2191
01:41:01.860 --> 01:41:03.000
It's under wraps right now,

2192
01:41:03.000 --> 01:41:05.790
but I'll definitely let you know when it's ready,

2193
01:41:05.790 --> 01:41:07.503
it probably won't be until 2023.

2194
01:41:08.700 --> 01:41:11.590
So what is it that you want

2195
01:41:12.441 --> 01:41:15.600
people to get out of "Bittersweet" the most?

2196
01:41:15.600 --> 01:41:19.683
Like if they're to take away one core idea.

2197
01:41:23.578 --> 01:41:25.320
I mean, I guess I'd say the idea of that,

2198
01:41:25.320 --> 01:41:27.210
whatever pain you can't get rid of

2199
01:41:27.210 --> 01:41:29.313
to make that your creative offering.

2200
01:41:31.650 --> 01:41:33.630
And then another large idea I would say,

2201
01:41:33.630 --> 01:41:37.323
and this applies to "Quiet" as well as to "Bittersweet" is,

2202
01:41:39.330 --> 01:41:43.050
the idea of there being ways of being in this world

2203
01:41:43.050 --> 01:41:44.970
that are not talked about

2204
01:41:44.970 --> 01:41:47.910
that are actually kind of hidden superpowers.

2205
01:41:47.910 --> 01:41:50.250
'Cause I hear now from so many people,

2206
01:41:50.250 --> 01:41:51.997
like the letters that I'm getting from people who have read

2207
01:41:51.997 --> 01:41:56.250
"Bittersweet" are so similar to the letters

2208
01:41:56.250 --> 01:41:58.230
I've gotten all these years from "Quiet" readers.

2209
01:41:58.230 --> 01:42:00.690
Like it's all these people saying,

2210
01:42:00.690 --> 01:42:04.200
I felt this all my life and either I never read words for it

2211
01:42:04.200 --> 01:42:06.180
or I never felt like it was okay to talk about

2212
01:42:06.180 --> 01:42:09.333
because it's not seen as the right way to be.

2213
01:42:12.300 --> 01:42:14.340
And yet there is a sense of it being a superpower

2214
01:42:14.340 --> 01:42:15.990
once you understand it as such.

2215
01:42:15.990 --> 01:42:16.823
Yeah.

2216
01:42:17.730 --> 01:42:21.790
I think it would be helpful to also share some words for

2217
01:42:23.220 --> 01:42:24.810
perhaps the extroverts out there

2218
01:42:24.810 --> 01:42:26.610
that have introverts in their lives.

2219
01:42:26.610 --> 01:42:29.010
Like maybe they have a kid who's introverted,

2220
01:42:29.010 --> 01:42:33.900
and they're struggling to understand why this person,

2221
01:42:33.900 --> 01:42:36.690
wants to be in their room and doesn't wanna go to the party.

2222
01:42:36.690 --> 01:42:39.600
Or I think from a parenting perspective,

2223
01:42:39.600 --> 01:42:42.360
I have a child who's very introverted

2224
01:42:42.360 --> 01:42:44.400
and it's been an education and a learning experience

2225
01:42:44.400 --> 01:42:48.600
to like even though I have introverted proclivities,

2226
01:42:48.600 --> 01:42:49.950
I still have some extra...

2227
01:42:49.950 --> 01:42:52.020
It's sort of like, I don't want to judge my child

2228
01:42:52.020 --> 01:42:53.910
because this is my child's choice

2229
01:42:53.910 --> 01:42:56.880
or this is what this person needs

2230
01:42:56.880 --> 01:42:59.760
to recharge the battery in terms of like,

2231
01:42:59.760 --> 01:43:02.280
how we parent and how we think about

2232
01:43:02.280 --> 01:43:04.680
supporting the introverts in our life.

2233
01:43:04.680 --> 01:43:06.900
Oh yeah, I mean, there's so much to say there,

2234
01:43:06.900 --> 01:43:09.480
but I mean, the first thing as you're saying is,

2235
01:43:09.480 --> 01:43:11.940
to just really understand like where your child

2236
01:43:11.940 --> 01:43:14.580
or spouse or whomever it is, is coming from,

2237
01:43:14.580 --> 01:43:16.280
and what do they need to be happy.

2238
01:43:19.800 --> 01:43:22.590
With children I think the fear often is that

2239
01:43:22.590 --> 01:43:25.110
if they're not kind of out there socializing enough

2240
01:43:25.110 --> 01:43:28.050
that they're not gonna like get enough of the bounties

2241
01:43:28.050 --> 01:43:29.400
that life has to offer.

2242
01:43:29.400 --> 01:43:32.820
So one thing to know is that like the research shows that

2243
01:43:32.820 --> 01:43:35.760
if your child has one or two close friends,

2244
01:43:35.760 --> 01:43:36.750
that's what they need.

2245
01:43:36.750 --> 01:43:38.280
Like if your kid has no friends at all,

2246
01:43:38.280 --> 01:43:39.960
that's when you should be concerned.

2247
01:43:39.960 --> 01:43:41.730
But if they have just a few friends,

2248
01:43:41.730 --> 01:43:44.223
and if they're happy with that, then that's fine.

2249
01:43:51.317 --> 01:43:55.448
I mean, for all introverts, there's the question of

2250
01:43:55.448 --> 01:43:57.930
if a person wants to stay home from a party or something,

2251
01:43:57.930 --> 01:43:59.760
is that because of a true preference

2252
01:43:59.760 --> 01:44:01.380
of how they wanna spend their time,

2253
01:44:01.380 --> 01:44:03.600
or is it out of a sense of fear or discomfort?

2254
01:44:03.600 --> 01:44:04.470
Sure, right.

2255
01:44:04.470 --> 01:44:06.210
And like with a child,

2256
01:44:06.210 --> 01:44:07.770
if you think it's fear or discomfort,

2257
01:44:07.770 --> 01:44:11.190
then that's when you can be a really helpful parent

2258
01:44:11.190 --> 01:44:14.790
in helping them to develop strategies and work through that.

2259
01:44:14.790 --> 01:44:15.810
But if it's just preference

2260
01:44:15.810 --> 01:44:17.700
of how they wanna spend their time,

2261
01:44:17.700 --> 01:44:19.890
then you wanna make sure they don't become too isolated,

2262
01:44:19.890 --> 01:44:21.600
but you also wanna honor their preference.

2263
01:44:21.600 --> 01:44:24.900
Yeah, that becomes very nuanced and difficult

2264
01:44:24.900 --> 01:44:28.050
because is it a preference or is it just,

2265
01:44:28.050 --> 01:44:30.187
you're afraid of trying something new?

2266
01:44:30.187 --> 01:44:33.834
Like you need to get pushed a little bit out into the world,

2267
01:44:33.834 --> 01:44:38.340
and learn new things and try things and fail and all of that

2268
01:44:38.340 --> 01:44:40.830
to Susan David's point of emotional resilience.

2269
01:44:40.830 --> 01:44:44.220
Like you can't procure that in a vacuum.

2270
01:44:44.220 --> 01:44:45.870
Yeah, and the answer to that,

2271
01:44:45.870 --> 01:44:48.090
like if you sense that it's fear or discomfort,

2272
01:44:48.090 --> 01:44:49.890
that's standing in the way,

2273
01:44:49.890 --> 01:44:51.180
the answer to that for parenting,

2274
01:44:51.180 --> 01:44:55.920
but also for our own selves is to expose your child

2275
01:44:55.920 --> 01:44:57.720
or yourself to the thing that you fear,

2276
01:44:57.720 --> 01:45:01.150
but to do it in very small steps so that it's manageable

2277
01:45:02.130 --> 01:45:04.500
so your child has small wins as they go.

2278
01:45:04.500 --> 01:45:07.353
So like a lot of introverted kids just for example,

2279
01:45:09.060 --> 01:45:10.470
are a little iffy about swimming

2280
01:45:10.470 --> 01:45:12.240
when they're first introduced.

2281
01:45:12.240 --> 01:45:15.990
And the answer is not to like throw them in the water.

2282
01:45:15.990 --> 01:45:17.550
It's more to say, okay,

2283
01:45:17.550 --> 01:45:20.040
let's go to the pool on a day when it's super quiet,

2284
01:45:20.040 --> 01:45:22.320
we'll go like late on a Tuesday evening,

2285
01:45:22.320 --> 01:45:25.170
and you're gonna dip your toe in the water, and that's it.

2286
01:45:25.170 --> 01:45:27.480
And then we're gonna celebrate and we're gonna go home.

2287
01:45:27.480 --> 01:45:30.240
And then you go back the next day and you do a little more

2288
01:45:30.240 --> 01:45:31.530
and a little more and a little more,

2289
01:45:31.530 --> 01:45:34.080
and little by little, they come to love it.

2290
01:45:34.080 --> 01:45:36.180
And then you can't tell the difference between them

2291
01:45:36.180 --> 01:45:38.640
and the kid who just jumped in from the beginning.

2292
01:45:38.640 --> 01:45:41.820
But it's like, a lot of times,

2293
01:45:41.820 --> 01:45:45.090
introverted kids need a longer runway that they travel down

2294
01:45:45.090 --> 01:45:47.550
before they take off and fly,

2295
01:45:47.550 --> 01:45:50.670
and they need to know that you are with them on the runway

2296
01:45:50.670 --> 01:45:53.430
and admiring them and respecting them

2297
01:45:53.430 --> 01:45:54.660
as they travel that runway.

2298
01:45:54.660 --> 01:45:55.493
Right.

2299
01:45:55.493 --> 01:45:57.060
Yeah, that's really good advice.

2300
01:45:57.060 --> 01:45:58.620
I like that.

2301
01:45:58.620 --> 01:46:03.040
The final thing I wanted to ask you about is this idea

2302
01:46:04.065 --> 01:46:08.520
of being so good in your particular role,

2303
01:46:08.520 --> 01:46:11.400
professionally as an introvert,

2304
01:46:11.400 --> 01:46:14.400
but inevitably getting promoted into a role

2305
01:46:14.400 --> 01:46:18.030
that requires extroversion, which is the story of your life.

2306
01:46:18.030 --> 01:46:20.310
Like to write this book about,

2307
01:46:20.310 --> 01:46:22.710
introversion, to be an introvert,

2308
01:46:22.710 --> 01:46:26.700
and then to become the public facing persona

2309
01:46:26.700 --> 01:46:29.580
behind this idea and this movement,

2310
01:46:29.580 --> 01:46:32.250
have foisted you onto the public stage where

2311
01:46:32.250 --> 01:46:37.250
now it's kind of your job to like be extroverted in public.

2312
01:46:37.890 --> 01:46:40.830
And having to kind of learn a new set of skills.

2313
01:46:40.830 --> 01:46:45.240
And I think it's common probably in less extreme examples

2314
01:46:45.240 --> 01:46:47.670
of people who excel at their job,

2315
01:46:47.670 --> 01:46:50.070
and then suddenly they get promoted into a role that

2316
01:46:50.070 --> 01:46:53.310
requires a completely different skill set that is

2317
01:46:53.310 --> 01:46:56.760
at odds with who they wanna be or what they're good at.

2318
01:46:56.760 --> 01:46:58.200
Yeah, okay.

2319
01:46:58.200 --> 01:47:00.693
So I mean, the first thing I would say is that,

2320
01:47:02.520 --> 01:47:06.180
when that happens, that's really the moment when you know,

2321
01:47:06.180 --> 01:47:09.420
whether you love and care about your work or not,

2322
01:47:09.420 --> 01:47:12.150
because it's one thing to have to go outside

2323
01:47:12.150 --> 01:47:15.210
your comfort zone in the service of work that you love.

2324
01:47:15.210 --> 01:47:17.250
And it's another thing to have to do it

2325
01:47:17.250 --> 01:47:20.370
for a job that you don't actually care about that much.

2326
01:47:20.370 --> 01:47:23.790
So like, I care so much about what I'm doing

2327
01:47:23.790 --> 01:47:24.990
and I love writing so much.

2328
01:47:24.990 --> 01:47:28.620
And these ideas that it's like worth it to me

2329
01:47:28.620 --> 01:47:32.583
to go through the parts that are more of a stretch.

2330
01:47:34.680 --> 01:47:37.980
So in some ways introverts can see that as an advantage

2331
01:47:37.980 --> 01:47:41.580
that they're less likely to get stuck in the wrong job

2332
01:47:41.580 --> 01:47:44.250
because it's just gonna be too painful at the end of the day

2333
01:47:44.250 --> 01:47:45.633
when they get promoted.

2334
01:47:47.310 --> 01:47:51.090
But also, so the psychologist, Brian Little,

2335
01:47:51.090 --> 01:47:53.410
talks about this a lot, the idea that

2336
01:47:55.650 --> 01:48:00.000
in the service of work or people we care about,

2337
01:48:00.000 --> 01:48:04.290
we can and do and should sometimes step out of character,

2338
01:48:04.290 --> 01:48:08.280
but you have to do it in a way that honors your own self.

2339
01:48:08.280 --> 01:48:10.710
And you have to give yourself lots of restorative niches

2340
01:48:10.710 --> 01:48:11.970
to come back.

2341
01:48:11.970 --> 01:48:15.183
So like, I do that all the time.

2342
01:48:15.183 --> 01:48:17.430
Like when I go out on trips,

2343
01:48:17.430 --> 01:48:19.740
and I'm giving talks or interviews or whatever,

2344
01:48:19.740 --> 01:48:23.280
I spend so much time afterwards ordering room service

2345
01:48:23.280 --> 01:48:25.290
and just like chilling in the hotel.

2346
01:48:25.290 --> 01:48:26.340
Yeah, yeah.

2347
01:48:26.340 --> 01:48:28.080
Yeah, it's like you can't wait to get back

2348
01:48:28.080 --> 01:48:31.320
to the hotel room and be alone.

2349
01:48:31.320 --> 01:48:32.850
Yes, yeah.

2350
01:48:32.850 --> 01:48:35.277
And I really like honor that time,

2351
01:48:35.277 --> 01:48:37.293
and I guard it really carefully.

2352
01:48:38.160 --> 01:48:40.560
And then I feel like I'm lucky

2353
01:48:40.560 --> 01:48:43.560
'cause I also get to spend a lot of time doing my work

2354
01:48:43.560 --> 01:48:46.170
is just like sitting at a cafe writing.

2355
01:48:46.170 --> 01:48:51.000
So I do think it's worth asking oneself

2356
01:48:51.000 --> 01:48:53.700
like if you get to a point where your work is having you

2357
01:48:53.700 --> 01:48:57.090
constantly doing things that are outside your comfort zone,

2358
01:48:57.090 --> 01:48:59.520
it might be the wrong place for you.

2359
01:48:59.520 --> 01:49:02.070
But if you can get it to a manageable place,

2360
01:49:02.070 --> 01:49:04.110
and it's in the service of something you care about,

2361
01:49:04.110 --> 01:49:05.460
then it's okay.

2362
01:49:05.460 --> 01:49:10.460
Yeah, if you being like this public facing person

2363
01:49:11.520 --> 01:49:14.880
on the subject of bittersweetness or introversion,

2364
01:49:14.880 --> 01:49:17.910
meant that you couldn't write anymore,

2365
01:49:17.910 --> 01:49:20.220
because now you're running a large organization,

2366
01:49:20.220 --> 01:49:22.320
that's trying to foster these ideas.

2367
01:49:22.320 --> 01:49:25.320
That probably would not be something you would want,

2368
01:49:25.320 --> 01:49:27.930
that would be too high of a cost.

2369
01:49:27.930 --> 01:49:28.763
Way too high of a cost.

2370
01:49:28.763 --> 01:49:30.840
Even if it would make you super successful,

2371
01:49:30.840 --> 01:49:33.900
and push these initiatives through and change the world,

2372
01:49:33.900 --> 01:49:36.960
it's not within your constitution to do so.

2373
01:49:36.960 --> 01:49:38.089
Yeah, exactly-

2374
01:49:38.089 --> 01:49:39.900
And having that self-awareness to recognize that.

2375
01:49:39.900 --> 01:49:41.463
Exactly, exactly.

2376
01:49:42.600 --> 01:49:45.180
I feel like it's not my constitution, it's not my calling.

2377
01:49:45.180 --> 01:49:47.793
It's not what I feel like I should be doing.

2378
01:49:50.040 --> 01:49:52.810
Yeah, I am super careful about that

2379
01:49:54.302 --> 01:49:56.430
when I go and do these public facing things.

2380
01:49:56.430 --> 01:49:59.760
And is it non-anxiety producing for you now

2381
01:49:59.760 --> 01:50:01.920
because you're so practiced at it?

2382
01:50:01.920 --> 01:50:06.030
So it's not like extremely an anxiety producing anymore

2383
01:50:06.030 --> 01:50:08.730
the way it used to be for me, really not at all.

2384
01:50:08.730 --> 01:50:12.093
I feel like I've overcome that, but I would still say that,

2385
01:50:13.560 --> 01:50:17.430
a morning in which I'm looking at a day full of publicity

2386
01:50:17.430 --> 01:50:21.510
versus a morning where I'm looking at a day full of writing

2387
01:50:21.510 --> 01:50:23.160
with my laptop,

2388
01:50:23.160 --> 01:50:24.468
those were completely different mornings-

2389
01:50:24.468 --> 01:50:25.347
Two different experiences.

2390
01:50:25.347 --> 01:50:26.180
Yeah, of course.

2391
01:50:26.180 --> 01:50:27.013
Two different experiences, yeah.

2392
01:50:27.013 --> 01:50:29.010
I don't know, maybe everybody feels that way.

2393
01:50:29.010 --> 01:50:31.890
But I feel like it's probably more intense...

2394
01:50:31.890 --> 01:50:33.240
The difference between those two

2395
01:50:33.240 --> 01:50:35.567
is probably more intense for me.

2396
01:50:35.567 --> 01:50:37.950
Like I think I read actually that you,

2397
01:50:37.950 --> 01:50:38.783
I'm I right about this,

2398
01:50:38.783 --> 01:50:43.320
that you spend your mornings doing exercise and,

2399
01:50:43.320 --> 01:50:45.210
writing and contemplative things.

2400
01:50:45.210 --> 01:50:46.590
The first part of the day.

2401
01:50:46.590 --> 01:50:48.570
Yeah, and then you do podcast interviews

2402
01:50:48.570 --> 01:50:50.010
and things like that later.

2403
01:50:50.010 --> 01:50:52.410
And when I read that, I thought that's really interesting.

2404
01:50:52.410 --> 01:50:54.690
I don't think that would work for me because,

2405
01:50:54.690 --> 01:50:59.370
if I knew that I had the public facing side

2406
01:50:59.370 --> 01:51:00.990
happening later in the day,

2407
01:51:00.990 --> 01:51:05.010
I don't know that I would be able to enjoy as much

2408
01:51:05.010 --> 01:51:06.949
the writing and contemplate stuff.

2409
01:51:06.949 --> 01:51:09.222
It spoils the other stuff earlier in the day.

2410
01:51:09.222 --> 01:51:10.380
Yeah, yeah.

2411
01:51:10.380 --> 01:51:11.400
That makes sense.

2412
01:51:11.400 --> 01:51:13.750
But it sounds like you don't experience that.

2413
01:51:15.300 --> 01:51:17.613
No, it definitely,

2414
01:51:18.900 --> 01:51:21.550
like public speaking creates a lot of anxiety for me,

2415
01:51:22.710 --> 01:51:24.990
but like doing podcasts less so,

2416
01:51:24.990 --> 01:51:27.870
and it's something I could do it in the morning too,

2417
01:51:27.870 --> 01:51:30.150
but I need that time for...

2418
01:51:30.150 --> 01:51:32.820
That's a recharge the battery time, right?

2419
01:51:32.820 --> 01:51:34.710
Like if I don't recharge the battery,

2420
01:51:34.710 --> 01:51:36.360
then when I come in to do this other stuff,

2421
01:51:36.360 --> 01:51:38.490
like I'm just not at my best.

2422
01:51:38.490 --> 01:51:42.150
So I have to protect that time for self-care.

2423
01:51:42.150 --> 01:51:44.490
And that doesn't mean that I always do it perfectly

2424
01:51:44.490 --> 01:51:46.740
or that I don't make exceptions,

2425
01:51:46.740 --> 01:51:48.480
'cause life intervenes and all of that.

2426
01:51:48.480 --> 01:51:50.580
But I try really hard to like,

2427
01:51:50.580 --> 01:51:53.910
that's how I preserve who I am,

2428
01:51:53.910 --> 01:51:54.960
I think, the whole thing.

2429
01:51:54.960 --> 01:51:57.750
And it's a privilege, not everybody can have that, you know?

2430
01:51:57.750 --> 01:51:58.583
Right, absolutely.

2431
01:51:58.583 --> 01:52:00.033
And I recognize that,

2432
01:52:01.530 --> 01:52:05.760
but it's pretty fundamental to kind of how I do things.

2433
01:52:05.760 --> 01:52:07.210
Yeah, no, that makes sense.

2434
01:52:08.070 --> 01:52:09.540
And I have my...

2435
01:52:09.540 --> 01:52:10.380
You'll appreciate this.

2436
01:52:10.380 --> 01:52:11.920
Like my home office

2437
01:52:13.860 --> 01:52:15.400
is a shipping container

2438
01:52:16.260 --> 01:52:18.480
that we kit it out into like an office.

2439
01:52:18.480 --> 01:52:19.800
So it's just like a writing office

2440
01:52:19.800 --> 01:52:21.330
with bookshelves and things like that.

2441
01:52:21.330 --> 01:52:23.100
And that's where I kind of can work at home.

2442
01:52:23.100 --> 01:52:26.970
So it's customized on the inside and has an air conditioner

2443
01:52:26.970 --> 01:52:27.803
and all that kind of stuff.

2444
01:52:27.803 --> 01:52:29.913
But it's literally a shipping container.

2445
01:52:31.648 --> 01:52:34.260
And when we were kind of figuring out

2446
01:52:34.260 --> 01:52:36.333
how to tweak it and customize it,

2447
01:52:37.260 --> 01:52:40.110
the kind of contractor that we hired to like help us with it

2448
01:52:40.110 --> 01:52:41.647
he's like, "Well, you're gonna want a big window

2449
01:52:41.647 --> 01:52:43.297
"and you should do, and it should be like this,

2450
01:52:43.297 --> 01:52:44.370
"let all the sunlight in."

2451
01:52:44.370 --> 01:52:46.737
I was like, "No, I don't want any window."

2452
01:52:47.850 --> 01:52:49.800
I literally want to have the experience

2453
01:52:49.800 --> 01:52:51.013
of being in the womb.

2454
01:52:51.013 --> 01:52:54.180
Like to feel on some level on a primal level,

2455
01:52:54.180 --> 01:52:56.940
like that's what makes me feel safe and protected.

2456
01:52:56.940 --> 01:52:58.440
Yeah, I know, I totally understand.

2457
01:52:58.440 --> 01:52:59.730
And then he still put a little window in,

2458
01:52:59.730 --> 01:53:02.703
and then I hung a shade over it and so I can black it out.

2459
01:53:03.630 --> 01:53:04.680
Anyway.

2460
01:53:04.680 --> 01:53:07.586
I hope that office designers are listening to that.

2461
01:53:07.586 --> 01:53:09.090
(Susan and Rich laughing)

2462
01:53:09.090 --> 01:53:10.410
'Cause they're all like, you're crazy,

2463
01:53:10.410 --> 01:53:11.250
you're gonna want this.

2464
01:53:11.250 --> 01:53:13.410
I'm like, I'm telling you I'm not gonna want it.

2465
01:53:13.410 --> 01:53:16.170
All right, well, any last thoughts for the

2466
01:53:16.170 --> 01:53:19.130
introverts out there grappling with bittersweetness?

2467
01:53:19.130 --> 01:53:21.609
(Susan and Rich laughing)

2468
01:53:21.609 --> 01:53:23.273
Before we wrap it up.

2469
01:53:23.273 --> 01:53:27.963
Yeah, I think we can wrap it up really just to understand.

2470
01:53:30.480 --> 01:53:31.890
I don't know, just that my view of life.

2471
01:53:31.890 --> 01:53:32.970
I don't know if I've said this already,

2472
01:53:32.970 --> 01:53:35.400
so edit this out if I have, but-

2473
01:53:35.400 --> 01:53:37.530
Lay down the truth right now.

2474
01:53:37.530 --> 01:53:40.680
My life philosophy is that there are

2475
01:53:40.680 --> 01:53:44.160
different kinds of superpowers that exist in this world.

2476
01:53:44.160 --> 01:53:47.067
And usually we're each only granted one or two of them.

2477
01:53:47.067 --> 01:53:49.140
And like we know this from all the movies, right?

2478
01:53:49.140 --> 01:53:50.997
There's like "Lightsabers,"

2479
01:53:52.101 --> 01:53:55.350
and then there's like "Spiderman" and "Incredible Hulks"

2480
01:53:55.350 --> 01:53:58.350
and all these different ways of being powerful in the world.

2481
01:53:58.350 --> 01:54:01.878
And so for people who are born with more of

2482
01:54:01.878 --> 01:54:04.137
a bitter sweet way of being, or an introverted way of being,

2483
01:54:04.137 --> 01:54:05.853
and a sensitive way of being,

2484
01:54:06.930 --> 01:54:11.250
these are superpowers that where you're like

2485
01:54:11.250 --> 01:54:12.420
in the stage of the movie,

2486
01:54:12.420 --> 01:54:14.640
you're like Luke Skywalker before he knows

2487
01:54:14.640 --> 01:54:16.500
that he has the force basically,

2488
01:54:16.500 --> 01:54:20.010
because your culture isn't teaching you about the superpower

2489
01:54:20.010 --> 01:54:22.920
that you naturally possess, but it's there.

2490
01:54:22.920 --> 01:54:25.440
It's just yours to discover.

2491
01:54:25.440 --> 01:54:27.390
I think there's something really powerful too,

2492
01:54:27.390 --> 01:54:31.350
about that quiet person who is really grounded.

2493
01:54:31.350 --> 01:54:34.290
There's a center of gravity and kind of an anchor

2494
01:54:34.290 --> 01:54:38.220
to that person who knows who he or she is.

2495
01:54:38.220 --> 01:54:41.520
And is really comfortable in that quiet space

2496
01:54:41.520 --> 01:54:44.400
that is perhaps exponentially

2497
01:54:44.400 --> 01:54:48.030
more powerful than the boisterous person who's,

2498
01:54:48.030 --> 01:54:53.030
really loud and perhaps charismatic, but unable to kind of

2499
01:54:55.830 --> 01:55:00.830
have the resonance that that quiet powerful person

2500
01:55:02.104 --> 01:55:02.937
can impart.
Yeah.

2501
01:55:02.937 --> 01:55:05.613
I think that's right, and like I often tell people,

2502
01:55:06.837 --> 01:55:09.720
'cause the quieter people are always concerned about,

2503
01:55:09.720 --> 01:55:12.690
how do you show up in a meeting and still have presence,

2504
01:55:12.690 --> 01:55:15.753
especially when everybody's talking, being very dominant?

2505
01:55:17.340 --> 01:55:20.520
But there's a way where if you're speaking from

2506
01:55:20.520 --> 01:55:23.493
a sense of conviction, people know it,

2507
01:55:23.493 --> 01:55:25.140
like they feel it right away,

2508
01:55:25.140 --> 01:55:27.060
and that's what carries the power.

2509
01:55:27.060 --> 01:55:30.450
So the answer is not to try to become

2510
01:55:30.450 --> 01:55:32.250
a louder, more dominant person,

2511
01:55:32.250 --> 01:55:35.130
the answer is more to get in the habit of understanding

2512
01:55:35.130 --> 01:55:38.400
what you truly believe, and speak what you truly believe.

2513
01:55:38.400 --> 01:55:40.410
And then people feel that.

2514
01:55:40.410 --> 01:55:41.880
That's where the power comes from.

2515
01:55:41.880 --> 01:55:44.073
There you go, beautiful.

2516
01:55:45.630 --> 01:55:47.250
Thank you.
Thank you.

2517
01:55:47.250 --> 01:55:48.660
No, I appreciate it.

2518
01:55:48.660 --> 01:55:51.330
The work that you do and the wisdom that you share

2519
01:55:51.330 --> 01:55:53.670
with the world is really powerful.

2520
01:55:53.670 --> 01:55:56.340
And it's obviously changing millions and millions

2521
01:55:56.340 --> 01:55:57.210
of people's lives.

2522
01:55:57.210 --> 01:56:02.210
And I think these are really important topics and subjects

2523
01:56:03.180 --> 01:56:06.360
that previously, perhaps we were too afraid to talk about

2524
01:56:06.360 --> 01:56:07.890
and you've made it a thing.

2525
01:56:07.890 --> 01:56:11.025
And I just wanted to publicly thank you for that.

2526
01:56:11.025 --> 01:56:11.858
Oh, my God.

2527
01:56:11.858 --> 01:56:12.990
And can't we just see what you do next.

2528
01:56:12.990 --> 01:56:15.240
Well, thank you so much and such a treat

2529
01:56:15.240 --> 01:56:16.620
to get to meet you in person.

2530
01:56:16.620 --> 01:56:18.780
And I just love the work you do.

2531
01:56:18.780 --> 01:56:19.908
So thank you.

2532
01:56:19.908 --> 01:56:22.080
Thank you, pleasure and the honor is mine.

2533
01:56:22.080 --> 01:56:24.300
So come and talk to me again sometime.

2534
01:56:24.300 --> 01:56:25.133
I would love it.

2535
01:56:25.133 --> 01:56:26.523
Cool, peace.

